environments.rawEnvironment:
\begin{exmp}[Admissible Orders in a Static Market]\label{exmp:adm-static}\index{admissible orders}\index{static market}
    Consider a simple static market, in which each order can be represented by $(p, q)$ where $p \in \mathbb{R}$ is the price\footnote{Classically, one often assumes prices to be positive, but in power markets prices may be negative. Real venues also impose product-specific price ticks and admissible price ranges; for European spot power examples, see \citet{epex2026tradingproducts,nemocommitteeSIDC}.} and $q \in [0,\infty)$ is the quantity\footnote{Like prices, actual order quantities are venue- and product-specific and may be restricted by minimum volume increments or lot sizes \citep{epex2026tradingproducts,nemocommitteeSIDC}.}. Each order can be classified as a buy or sell order, such that $((p,q), \kappa)$ is an order with direction $\kappa \in \{\mathrm{buy},~\mathrm{sell}\}$. As such, the space of admissible orders may be given by
    \begin{align*}
        \bigsqcup_{\kappa \in \{\mathrm{buy},~\mathrm{sell}\}} \mathbb{R}\times [0,\infty).
    \end{align*}
    However, in this example, there is no difference between using disjoint unions or the product space
    \begin{align*}
        \mathbb{R}\times [0,\infty) \times \{\mathrm{buy},~\mathrm{sell}\}.
    \end{align*}
    Indeed, one defines the map 
    \begin{align*}
        ((p,q), \kappa) \mapsto (p,q,\kappa),
    \end{align*}
    which is easily shown to be an isomorphism. In this example, one may view the disjoint union as a nuisance, but, as we shall later see, it has advantages.
\end{exmp}

