\begin{exmp}[Admissible Orders in a Dynamic Market]\label{exmp:adm-dyn}\index{dynamic market}
We now extend Example~\ref{exmp:adm-static} to the dynamic setting. Here each order includes the time at which it was submitted. The order can be given in three ways, as $((p,q),(\kappa, t))$, $((p,q,t),\kappa)$, or $(p,q,\kappa,t)$, where $p \in \mathbb{R}$ is the price, $q \in [0,\infty)$ is the quantity, $t\in [0,\infty)$ is the time of submission, and $\kappa \in \{\mathrm{buy},~\mathrm{sell}\}$ is the direction. In the case with no restrictions, one has bijections at the level of underlying sets. 
\end{exmp}

\begin{exmp}[Agent-Based Admissible Orders in a Dynamic Market]\label{exmp:agent-adm-dyn}
    We now extend Example~\ref{exmp:agent-adm-static} to the dynamic case. Once again, let $A$ be the set of agents within the market, and let $P_{\kappa, a, t}$ and $Q_{\kappa, a, t}$ be the sets of prices and quantities that are admissible for agent $a \in A$ at time $t \in [0,\infty)$ in direction $\kappa \in \{\mathrm{buy},~\mathrm{sell}\}$. Each order can be seen as an element of
    \begin{align*}
        \bigsqcup_{(\kappa, a, t) \in \{\mathrm{buy},~\mathrm{sell}\}\times A\times [0,\infty) } P_{\kappa, a, t}\times Q_{\kappa, a, t}.
    \end{align*}
    Again, the map
    \begin{align*}
        ((p,q),(\kappa, a, t)) \mapsto (p,q,\kappa,a,t),
    \end{align*}
    is injective. It gives the canonical injections
    \begin{align}
        &\bigsqcup_{(\kappa, a, t) \in \{\mathrm{buy},~\mathrm{sell}\}\times A\times [0,\infty) } P_{\kappa, a, t}\times Q_{\kappa, a, t}\label{eq:agent-adm-dyn-1} \\
        &\hookrightarrow  \bigsqcup_{(\kappa, a) \in \{\mathrm{buy},~\mathrm{sell}\}\times A} P_{\kappa, a}\times Q_{\kappa, a} \times [0,\infty)\label{eq:agent-adm-dyn-2}\\
        &\hookrightarrow \mathbb{R}\times[0,\infty) \times \{\mathrm{buy},~\mathrm{sell}\} \times A \times [0,\infty),\label{eq:agent-adm-dyn-3} 
    \end{align}
    where
    \begin{align*}
        P_{\kappa, a} \equiv \bigcup_{t\in [0,\infty)}P_{\kappa, a, t} \quad \quad Q_{\kappa, a}\equiv \bigcup_{t\in [0,\infty)}Q_{\kappa, a, t}.
    \end{align*}
    In general, the set in \eqref{eq:agent-adm-dyn-1} restricts the trading possibilities of each agent in each direction at each time, whereas \eqref{eq:agent-adm-dyn-2} does not have time-varying bounds for each agent and direction. These examples are relevant for deterministic time-varying restrictions, such as gate closure or time-specific price limits; state-dependent admissibility, for instance when an agent has already taken a short position and cannot sell short further because of margin constraints, is modelled as a constraint on actions given the current state. Finally, \eqref{eq:agent-adm-dyn-3} is the agent-labelled version of the unrestricted order space of Example~\ref{exmp:adm-dyn}.
\end{exmp}

