proof.rawEnvironment:
\begin{proof}
    We must show that there exists an isomorphism between the sets $\bigcup_{\alpha \in A} X_\alpha$ and $\bigsqcup_{\alpha \in A} X_\alpha$. Let $\iota : \bigsqcup_{\alpha \in A} X_\alpha \to \bigcup_{\alpha \in A} X_\alpha$ be the mapping defined by 
    \begin{align*}
        \iota(x,\alpha) = x.
    \end{align*}
    We show that $\iota$ is bijective. For injectivity, we require
    \begin{align*}
        \iota(x,\alpha) = \iota(x^\prime, \alpha^\prime) \implies (x, \alpha) = (x^\prime, \alpha^\prime),
    \end{align*}
    and indeed, if $\iota(x,\alpha) = \iota(x^\prime,\alpha^\prime)$, then $x = x^\prime$; since the sets are pairwise disjoint, $x \in X_\alpha \cap X_{\alpha^\prime}$ forces $\alpha = \alpha^\prime$. Hence $\iota$ is injective, and it is clearly surjective. Therefore, it is bijective and hence an isomorphism between the disjoint union and ordinary union, when the components are disjoint.
\end{proof}

