\begin{exmp}[Total Order for a Dynamic Market]\label{exmp:total-order-dynamic-market}
    We continue from Example~\ref{exmp:adm-dyn}, in which we considered the orders as elements of the set 
    \begin{align*}
        O = \bigsqcup_{\kappa \in \{\mathrm{buy},~\mathrm{sell}\}} \mathbb{R}\times [0,\infty) \times [0,\infty),  
    \end{align*}
    where the components of each $((p,q,t), \kappa) \in O$ represent the price, $p \in \mathbb{R}$, quantity, $q \in [0,\infty)$, time of submission, $t \in [0,\infty)$, and direction $\kappa \in \{\mathrm{buy},\mathrm{sell}\}$. Now, define the orders 
    \begin{align*}
        &(p,q,t) \preceq_{\mathrm{buy}} (p^\prime, q^\prime, t^\prime) \iff\\ &(p > p^\prime) \lor ((p = p^\prime) \land (t < t^\prime)) \lor ((p = p^\prime) \land (t = t^\prime) \land (q \geq q^\prime)),
    \end{align*}
    which ranks a buy order $(p,q,t)$ ahead of $(p^\prime, q^\prime, t^\prime)$ if it has a higher price; at equal prices, if it was submitted earlier; and at equal prices and times, if it is for a larger quantity. Similarly, we define 
    \begin{align*}
        &(p,q, t) \preceq_{\mathrm{sell}} (p^\prime, q^\prime,t^\prime) \iff \\
        &(p < p^\prime) \lor ((p = p^\prime) \land (t < t^\prime)) \lor ((p = p^\prime) \land (t = t^\prime) \land (q \geq q^\prime)),
    \end{align*}
    where sell orders are ranked increasingly according to price. That is, the ``best'' sell order is the one with the smallest price, but otherwise it is constructed analogously to the buy order. Endow $\{\mathrm{buy}, ~\mathrm{sell}\}$ with the order $\leq$ of Example~\ref{exmp:total-order-static-market}, for which
    \begin{align*}
        \kappa \leq \kappa^\prime \iff \kappa = \kappa^\prime \lor (\kappa = \mathrm{buy} \land \kappa^\prime = \mathrm{sell}).
    \end{align*}
    Since $\leq, \preceq_{\mathrm{buy}}$, and  $\preceq_{\mathrm{sell}}$ are all total orders, we have that the lexicographic order defined by
    \begin{align*}
        (o, \kappa) \preceq (o^\prime, \kappa^\prime) \iff \kappa < \kappa^\prime \lor (\kappa = \kappa^\prime \land o \preceq_\kappa o^\prime), 
    \end{align*}
    is total, where $<$ is the strict part of $\leq$ and $(o,\kappa),(o^\prime, \kappa^\prime) \in O$. 
\end{exmp}

