environments.rawEnvironment:
\begin{rem}
    Note that the ordering constructed in Example~\ref{exmp:completing-order-agent-based-static-market} acts on the equivalence classes in $O_{\sim}$ rather than on the individual agent-labelled orders in $O$. Heuristically, from a market perspective, if two distinct agents $a, a^\prime \in A$ submit orders with identical price $p$, quantity $q$, and direction $\kappa$, their submissions belong to the same equivalence class $[((p,q),\kappa,a)]_{\sim}$. Consequently, the quotient ordering does not induce a total order on the original space $O$, nor does it track the multiplicity of identical submissions or the individual authorisation sets $P_{\kappa,a} \times Q_{\kappa,a}$. In modelling variants where a total order on $O$ itself is required, for instance when certain market participants are assigned institutional priority, one may instead endow $A$ with a priority order and construct a lexicographic order on $O$ analogously to Example~\ref{exmp:total-order-static-market}.
\end{rem}

\begin{defn}[Disjoint-Union Metric]\label{defn:disjoint-union-metrisation}\index{disjoint union!metric}
    Let $(X_\alpha, d_\alpha)_{\alpha \in A}$ be a family of metric spaces and $X = \sqcup_{\alpha \in A} X_\alpha$. Define the mapping $d:X\times X\to [0,\infty]$ by
    \begin{align}\label{eq:disjoint-metric}
        d((x,\alpha), (x^\prime, \alpha^\prime)) = \begin{cases}
            d_\alpha(x,x^\prime) & \alpha = \alpha^\prime\\
            \infty & \alpha \neq \alpha^\prime.
        \end{cases}
    \end{align}
    We call $d$ the disjoint-union metric induced by $(d_\alpha)_{\alpha\in A}$, or simply the disjoint-union metric if the metrics $(d_\alpha)_{\alpha\in A}$ are clear from context. Moreover, we refer to $(X,d)$ as the disjoint union metric space. 
\end{defn}

