environments.rawEnvironment:
\begin{rem}
    Note that we allow the metric to have range $[0,\infty]$, as opposed to $[0,\infty)$, which is utilised in classical analysis. In some texts, our definition is that of an \emph{extended} metric, whereas a metric requires the range to be $[0,\infty)$. For our purposes, this has no topological implication, because one can define the finite-valued metric
    \begin{align*}
        \bar{d}(x,x^\prime) =\min(d(x,x^\prime),1)
    \end{align*}
    which induces the same topology as its extended-valued counterpart (see \citet{munkres2000topology}).
\end{rem}

