\begin{proof}
    We must show that the mapping $d:X\times X \to [0,\infty]$ satisfies the three criteria for all $(x,\alpha),(x^\prime, \alpha^\prime),(x^\ast, \alpha^\ast) \in X = \bigsqcup_{\alpha \in A} X_\alpha$:
    \begin{enumerate}[(i)]
        \item $d((x,\alpha), (x^\prime, \alpha^\prime)) = 0$ if and only if $(x,\alpha) = (x^\prime, \alpha^\prime)$.\label{eq:disjoint-union-metrisation-1}
        \item $d((x,\alpha), (x^\prime, \alpha^\prime)) = d((x^\prime, \alpha^\prime), (x,\alpha))$. \label{eq:disjoint-union-metrisation-2}
        \item $d((x,\alpha), (x^\prime, \alpha^\prime)) \leq d((x,\alpha), (x^\ast, \alpha^\ast)) + d((x^\ast, \alpha^\ast), (x^\prime, \alpha^\prime))$. \label{eq:disjoint-union-metrisation-3}
    \end{enumerate}
    The criteria \eqref{eq:disjoint-union-metrisation-1} and \eqref{eq:disjoint-union-metrisation-2} are trivial. For \eqref{eq:disjoint-union-metrisation-3}, we use the usual extended-real convention that $r\leq\infty$ for every $r\in[0,\infty]$. If $\alpha \neq \alpha^\prime$, the right-hand side of the inequality is infinite, since $\alpha^\ast$ cannot be equal to both $\alpha$ and $\alpha^\prime$. Hence, it remains to consider the case $\alpha = \alpha^\prime$. Again, if $\alpha^\ast \neq \alpha = \alpha^\prime$ the claim is trivial, so we only need to consider $\alpha^\ast = \alpha = \alpha^\prime$, but then it reduces to the standard triangle inequality for $d_\alpha$, which, by assumption, is a metric.
\end{proof}

