environments.rawEnvironment:
\begin{prop}\label{prop:disjoint-union-metrisation}
    Let $(X_\alpha, d_\alpha)_{\alpha \in A}$ be a family of metric spaces and let $X = \sqcup_{\alpha \in A} X_\alpha$. Then, $(X,d)$, where $d$ is the disjoint union metric, is a metric space. 
\end{prop}

proof.rawEnvironment:
\begin{proof}
    First, we show \eqref{property:disjoint-metric-1}. We must show that there is a countable and dense subset $U \subseteq X$. Now, by assumption each $(X_\alpha, d_\alpha)$ is separable. Then, by definition, there exists a countable and dense subset, say $U_\alpha$, for which 
    \begin{align*}
        \forall x_\alpha \in X_\alpha\ \forall \varepsilon> 0\ \exists u_\alpha \in U_\alpha : d_\alpha(x_\alpha, u_\alpha) < \varepsilon.
    \end{align*}
    We claim that the set
    \begin{align*}
        U = \bigsqcup_{\alpha \in A} U_\alpha
    \end{align*}
    is both countable and dense in $(X,d)$. First, note that 
    \begin{align*}
        U = \bigcup_{\alpha \in A} (U_\alpha \times \{\alpha\}).
    \end{align*}
    For each $\alpha \in A$, the set $U_\alpha \times \{\alpha\}$ is countable, as it naturally bijects with the countable set $U_\alpha$. Moreover, $\bigcup_{\alpha \in A} U_\alpha \times \{\alpha\}$ is a countable union of countable sets and therefore countable. Hence, $U$ is indeed countable. 

    To show that $U$ is dense in $X$, we must show that for every $\varepsilon > 0 $ and $x \in X$ there exists a $u\in U$ such that $d(x,u) < \varepsilon$. To this end, let $\varepsilon > 0$ and $x\in X$ be given. Notice that $x = (x_\alpha, \alpha)$ for some $\alpha \in A$ and $x_{\alpha} \in X_\alpha$. Since $U_\alpha$ is dense in $X_\alpha$, there exists an element $u_\alpha \in U_\alpha$ such that $d_\alpha(x_\alpha, u_\alpha) < \varepsilon$. Define $u = (u_\alpha, \alpha)$; then $u \in U$ and $d(x,u) = d_\alpha(x_\alpha,u_\alpha) < \varepsilon$, as desired. 

    Next, we must show that any Cauchy sequence in $(X,d)$ converges in $X$. To this end, recall that a sequence $(x_n)_{n\in\mathbb{N}} \subseteq X$ is said to be Cauchy if 
    \begin{align*}
        \forall \varepsilon > 0\ \exists N \in \mathbb{N}: m,n\geq N \implies d(x_n, x_m) < \varepsilon.
    \end{align*}
    Each $x \in X$ has the form $x = (x_\alpha, \alpha)$, and so the Cauchy sequence $(x_n)_{n\in \mathbb{N}}$ admits the representation 
    \begin{align*}
        x_n = (x_{\alpha_n, n}, \alpha_n).
    \end{align*}
    Moreover, since the sequence is Cauchy, choose $N \in \mathbb{N}$ such that $d(x_n,x_m)<1$ for all $n,m\geq N$. This finite bound implies $\alpha_n=\alpha_m$ for all $n,m\geq N$; set $\alpha=\alpha_N$. Now, for all tail indices, $d(x_n, x_m) = d_\alpha (x_{\alpha, n}, x_{\alpha,m})$, implying that the sequence $(x_{\alpha, n})_{n = N}^\infty$ is Cauchy in $X_\alpha$. By assumption, $(X_\alpha, d_\alpha)$ is complete, and so the sequence $(x_{\alpha, n})_{n = N}^\infty$ has a limit, say, $x_\alpha \in X_\alpha$. Then $x = (x_\alpha, \alpha) \in X$ and clearly $(x_n)_{n\in\mathbb{N}}$ converges to $x$. 
\end{proof}

