\begin{rem}\label{rem:hausdorff-convention}
    We use the convention that 
    \begin{align*}
        d_H(\emptyset,\xi) = d_H(\xi,\emptyset) = \begin{cases}
            0 & \xi = \emptyset\\
            \infty & \xi \neq \emptyset.
        \end{cases}
    \end{align*}
    Moreover, the Hausdorff distance is generally denoted by adding the $H$ subscript to the metric, i.e. $d_H$ if the inducing metric is $d$ or $\rho_H$ if the inducing metric is $\rho$. 
\end{rem}

