proof.rawEnvironment:
\begin{proof}
    Since $\mathscr{F}(X) \subseteq \wp(X)$ it follows by Proposition~\ref{prop:hausdorff-distance} that $(\mathscr{F}(X), d_H)$ is a pseudometric space. By Corollary~\ref{cor:pseudometric-metric-relation} it suffices to show that for all $\xi, \eta \in \mathscr{F}(X)$ it holds that 
    \begin{align*}
        d_H(\xi, \eta) = 0 \implies \xi = \eta. 
    \end{align*}
    First, note that the claim is trivial if either $\xi$ or $\eta$ is the empty set, following Remark~\ref{rem:hausdorff-convention}. Therefore, suppose that $\xi$ and $\eta$ are non-empty. Since the sets are finite, the infimum coincides with the minimum and similarly the supremum coincides with the maximum, so
    \begin{align*}
        \max_{x \in \xi} \min_{x^\prime \in \eta} d(x,x^\prime) = 0 \iff \xi \subseteq \eta.
    \end{align*}
    However, notice that $d_H(\xi, \eta) =0$ if and only if $\max_{x \in \xi} \min_{x^\prime \in \eta} d(x,x^\prime) = 0$ and $\max_{x^\prime \in \eta} \min_{x\in \xi} d(x,x^\prime) = 0$, which holds if and only if $\xi \subseteq \eta$ and $\eta \subseteq \xi$ respectively, and so it follows that $\xi = \eta$. 
\end{proof}

