    \begin{align*}
        \xi = \{(i,x(i)) \mid i \in I\}
    \end{align*}
    is the limit of the Cauchy sequence. For $k \geq N$ one has 
    \begin{align*}
        d_H^\ast(\xi_k, \xi) \leq \max_{i \in I} d(x_k(i), x(i)),
    \end{align*}
    since the same indexed point in $\xi$ is an admissible Hausdorff match for the corresponding indexed point in $\xi_k$, and conversely. The right-hand side corresponds to the maximum metric on a finite product of $X$. Therefore, one has that 
    \begin{align}
        \lim_{k\to\infty} d_H^\ast(\xi_k, \xi) = 0,
    \end{align}
    since $x_k(i) \to x(i)$ for each of the finitely many $i \in I$, which proves the claim.

