\begin{cor}\label{thm:sorting-exists}
    Let $(X,\preceq)$ be a partially ordered set. Then, there exists at least one sorting map $\varsigma:\mathscr{T}(X) \to \mathscr{S}(X,\preceq)$. 
\end{cor}

proof.rawEnvironment:
\begin{proof}
    Existence follows from Corollary~\ref{thm:sorting-exists}. To prove uniqueness, let $\varsigma,\varsigma^\prime:\mathscr{T}(X)\to \mathscr{S}(X,\preceq)$ be sorting maps and fix $\bm{x}\in\mathscr{T}(X)$. Set
    \begin{align*}
        \bm{y} = \varsigma(\bm{x})
        \qquad \text{and} \qquad
        \bm{y}^\prime = \varsigma^\prime(\bm{x}).
    \end{align*}
    Both $\bm{y}$ and $\bm{y}^\prime$ are sorted tuples obtained from $\bm{x}$ by permutations, so they have the same length and the same entries, counted with multiplicity.

    Suppose for contradiction that $\bm{y} \neq \bm{y}^\prime$. Then there exists a smallest index $k \in \mathrm{domain}(\bm{x})$ such that
    \begin{align*}
        \bm{y}(k) \neq \bm{y}^\prime(k).
    \end{align*}
    By totality, either $\bm{y}(k) \prec \bm{y}^\prime(k)$ or $\bm{y}^\prime(k) \prec \bm{y}(k)$. Swapping the roles of $\bm{y}$ and $\bm{y}^\prime$ if necessary, we may assume that
    \begin{align*}
        \bm{y}(k) \prec \bm{y}^\prime(k).
    \end{align*}
    Since $\bm{y}$ and $\bm{y}^\prime$ contain the same entries with the same multiplicities, and since by minimality of $k$ they agree on $\{1,\dots,k-1\}$, their entries on $\{k,\dots,\ell(\bm{x})\}$ coincide as multisets. Hence there exists an index $m \geq k$, and in fact $m > k$ because $\bm{y}^\prime(k) \neq \bm{y}(k)$, such that
    \begin{align*}
        \bm{y}^\prime(m) = \bm{y}(k).
    \end{align*}
    However, $\bm{y}^\prime$ is sorted, so $k < m$ implies
    \begin{align*}
        \bm{y}^\prime(k) \preceq \bm{y}^\prime(m) = \bm{y}(k),
    \end{align*}
    contradicting $\bm{y}(k) \prec \bm{y}^\prime(k)$. Hence $\bm{y} = \bm{y}^\prime$. Since $\bm{x}$ was arbitrary, the sorting map is unique.

    For $\bm{x}\in \mathscr{S}(X,\preceq)$, the identity permutation already sorts $\bm{x}$, so the argument above gives $\varsigma(\bm{x}) = \bm{x}$; that is, $\varsigma$ acts as the identity on $\mathscr{S}(X,\preceq)$, and hence $\varsigma\circ\varsigma = \varsigma$.

    Finally, let $\rho:\mathrm{domain}(\bm{x})\to\mathrm{domain}(\bm{x})$ be a bijection. Then $\varsigma(\bm{x}\circ\rho)$ and $\varsigma(\bm{x})$ are both sorted tuples obtained from $\bm{x}$ by permutations, and the argument above shows that two such tuples coincide; hence $\varsigma(\bm{x}\circ\rho)=\varsigma(\bm{x})$.
\end{proof}

