\begin{proof}
    If $\bm{x} = \bm{e}$, then $\Gamma(\bm{x}) = \emptyset$ and $\kappa = 0$, so both claims hold vacuously. Suppose therefore that $\ell(\bm{x}) > 0$.

    First, we show (i). We must show that $\bigcup_{k=1}^{\kappa} K_k = \Gamma(\bm{x})$ and that $K_k \cap K_{k^\prime} = \emptyset$ for all $k, k^\prime \in \{1,\dots,\kappa\}$ with $k \neq k^\prime$. By Proposition~\ref{prop:sim-equivalence}, the connectivity relation $\overset{\bm{x}}{\sim}$ is an equivalence relation on $\mathrm{domain}(\bm{x})$, so its equivalence classes $C_1,\dots,C_\kappa$ form a partition of $\mathrm{domain}(\bm{x})$. Consequently,
    \begin{align*}
        \bigcup_{k=1}^{\kappa} K_k
        = \bigcup_{k=1}^{\kappa} \{(i,\bm{x}(i)) \in \Gamma(\bm{x}) \mid i \in C_k\}
        = \left\{(i,\bm{x}(i)) \in \Gamma(\bm{x}) \;\middle|\; i \in \bigcup_{k=1}^{\kappa} C_k\right\}
        = \Gamma(\bm{x}).
    \end{align*}
    Moreover, if $k \neq k^\prime$, then $C_k \cap C_{k^\prime} = \emptyset$, and since the first coordinate of any element in $K_k$ lies in $C_k$, it follows that $K_k \cap K_{k^\prime} = \emptyset$.

    Next, we show (ii). Let $k, k^\prime \in \{1,\dots,\kappa\}$ with $k \neq k^\prime$, and let $(i,\bm{x}(i)) \in K_k$ and $(j,\bm{x}(j)) \in K_{k^\prime}$ be arbitrary. We must show that $\neg(\bm{x}(i) \preceq \bm{x}(j))$ and $\neg(\bm{x}(j) \preceq \bm{x}(i))$. Suppose for contradiction that $\bm{x}(i) \preceq \bm{x}(j)$ or $\bm{x}(j) \preceq \bm{x}(i)$. By Definition~\ref{defn:index-comparable}, this means that $i \overset{\bm{x}}{\leftrightsquigarrow} j$, and hence $i \overset{\bm{x}}{\sim} j$ by Definition~\ref{defn:connectivity-relation}. Since $i \in C_k$ and $j \in C_{k^\prime}$, it follows that $C_k = C_{k^\prime}$, which contradicts $k \neq k^\prime$. Therefore, $\bm{x}(i)$ and $\bm{x}(j)$ are incomparable in $X$, as desired.
\end{proof}

