\begin{proof}
    Recall that by definition we have 
    \begin{align*}
        \mathscr{L}(X) = \{\xi \in \mathscr{F}(\mathbb{N} \times X) \mid \forall n \in \mathbb{N}: |(\{n\} \times X) \cap \xi| \leq 1\},
    \end{align*}
    which means that for any $n \in \mathbb{N}$, there is at most one element of $\xi$ whose first coordinate is $n$. Therefore, if $(n,x)$ and $(n,y)$ are both in $\xi$, then we must have $x = y$, and hence the restriction of $\Pi_{\mathbb{N}}$ to $\xi$ is injective.
\end{proof}

