    \end{align*}
    as a shorthand for the concatenation of the tuples $\bm{x}_1, \dots, \bm{x}_j$, where $\oplus$ is the concatenation operator for tuples ($\bm{x}\oplus\bm{y}$ lists the entries of $\bm{x}$ followed by those of $\bm{y}$; formally, see Definition~\ref{defn:concat-tuple}). Here, the order is important, as the operation is not commutative.

proof.rawEnvironment:
\begin{proof}
    First, note that $\varsigma_{\mathrm{min}}(\bm{e}) = \bm{e}$, which is sorted by definition. Let $\bm{x} \in \mathscr{T}(X)$ with $\ell(\bm{x}) > 0$, and write
    \begin{align*}
        \mathcal{K}(\bm{x}) = \{K_1,\dots,K_\kappa\},
    \end{align*}
    where the components are ordered by first appearance. Since each $K_r$ is a labelled finite set, the tuple $\varsigma(K_r)$ is well-defined by Definition~\ref{defn:sorting-finite-sets}, and so is the concatenation
    \begin{align*}
        \varsigma_{\mathrm{min}}(\bm{x}) = \bigoplus_{r=1}^{\kappa} \varsigma(K_r).
    \end{align*}

    Each block $\varsigma(K_r)$ is sorted by construction. Moreover, if $r < s$, then every element of $K_r$ is incomparable with every element of $K_s$ by Proposition~\ref{prop:components-partition-incomp}. Hence no element from the later block can be strictly smaller than an element from the earlier block. Therefore, the concatenation of the sorted blocks is again sorted, so $\varsigma_{\mathrm{min}}(\bm{x}) \in \mathscr{S}(X,\preceq)$.

    It remains to verify the permutation property. For each component $K_r$, the tuple $\pi(K_r)$ produced in Definition~\ref{defn:sorting-finite-sets} lists exactly the indices appearing in $K_r$, in the order used by $\varsigma(K_r)$. In particular, by Lemma~\ref{lem:labelled-finite-set-natural-projection-injective}, $\pi(K_r)$ is a bijection from $\{1,\dots,|K_r|\}$ onto the index set
    \begin{align*}
        I_r = \Pi_{\mathbb{N}}(K_r).
    \end{align*}
    Since the family $\mathcal{K}(\bm{x})$ is a partition of $\Gamma(\bm{x})$, the sets $I_1,\dots,I_\kappa$ form a partition of $\mathrm{domain}(\bm{x})$. Consequently, the concatenation
    \begin{align*}
        \sigma = \bigoplus_{r = 1}^{\kappa} \pi(K_r)
    \end{align*}
    has length $\ell(\bm{x})$ and image $\mathrm{domain}(\bm{x})$. Because the images of the blocks are pairwise disjoint, $\sigma:\mathrm{domain}(\bm{x}) \to \mathrm{domain}(\bm{x})$ is bijective.

    By construction, the tuple $\bm{x}\circ \sigma$ first lists the entries from $K_1$ in the order prescribed by $\varsigma(K_1)$, then the entries from $K_2$ in the order prescribed by $\varsigma(K_2)$, and so on. Hence
    \begin{align*}
        \bm{x}\circ\sigma = \bigoplus_{r=1}^{\kappa} \varsigma(K_r) = \varsigma_{\mathrm{min}}(\bm{x}),
    \end{align*}
    which proves that $\varsigma_{\mathrm{min}}$ is a sorting map.
\end{proof}

