\begin{proof}
    First, note that if $f$ is measurable, then $f\restriction_{X_n}$ is immediately seen to be measurable by definition of the trace $\sigma$-algebra. Therefore, we will prove that if $f\restriction_{X_n}$ is measurable for each $n\in \mathbb{N}$, then $f$ is measurable. 

    That is, for each $B\in\mathcal{G}$ we must show that 
    \begin{align*}
        \forall n \in \mathbb{N}: f\restriction_{X_n}^{-1}(B) \in \mathcal{F}_n \implies f^{-1}(B) \in \mathcal{F}.  
    \end{align*}
    But note that 
    \begin{align*}
        f^{-1}(B) &= \{x \in X \mid f(x) \in B\}\\
        &= \bigcup_{n\in \mathbb{N}}\{x \in X_n \mid f(x) \in B\}\\
        &=\bigcup_{n\in\mathbb{N}} f\restriction_{X_n}^{-1}(B) \\
        &= \bigcup_{n\in\mathbb{N}} (U_n \cap X_n),
    \end{align*}
    where $U_n \in \mathcal{F}$ by the definition of the trace $\sigma$-algebra, and $U_n \cap X_n \in \mathcal{F}$ because $X_n \in \mathcal{F}$. Consequently, $f^{-1}(B) \in \mathcal{F}$, as $\mathcal{F}$ is closed under countable unions.
\end{proof}

