\begin{thm}\label{thm:measurable-partition}
    Let $(X,\mathcal{F})$ and $(Y, \mathcal{G})$ be measurable spaces. Let $(X_n)_{n\in\mathbb{N}}$ be a measurable partition\index{measurable partition} of $X$. That is, for each $n\in \mathbb{N}$ one has $X_n \in \mathcal{F}$, and for $n,m \in\mathbb{N}$ with $n \neq m$ it holds that $X_n \cap X_m =\emptyset$, and  
    \begin{align*}
        \bigcup_{n\in\mathbb{N}} X_n =X. 
    \end{align*}
    Then any function $f:X \to Y$ is $\mathcal{F}/\mathcal{G}$-measurable if and only if $f\restriction_{X_n} :X_n \to Y$ is $\mathcal{F}_n/\mathcal{G}$-measurable for all $n \in \mathbb{N}$, where $\mathcal{F}_n = \{ B \cap X_n \mid B \in \mathcal{F}\}$ is the trace $\sigma$-algebra. 
\end{thm}

