\begin{proof}
    First, note that $\mathbb{N}$ is countable. Moreover, for any $n \in \mathbb{N}_0$ the set $\mathbb{N}^n$ is countable, as a finite product of countable sets is countable. Finally, 
    \begin{align*}
        \mathscr{T}(\mathbb{N})=\bigcup_{n \in \mathbb{N}_0} \mathbb{N}^n
    \end{align*}
    is a countable union of countable sets and hence countable. As $\mathscr{P}\subseteq \mathscr{T}(\mathbb{N})$ is a subset of a countable set, it is countable.
\end{proof}

