\begin{proof}
    We must show that $\bigcup_{n \in \mathbb{N}} \mathscr{U}_n = \mathscr{T}(X)$ and that $\mathscr{U}_n \cap \mathscr{U}_k = \emptyset$ for all $n, k \in \mathbb{N}$ with $n \neq k$.

    First, since $\mathscr{U}_n \subseteq \mathscr{V}_{\pi_{(n)}} \subseteq \mathscr{T}(X)$ for every $n \in \mathbb{N}$, it holds that $\bigcup_{n \in \mathbb{N}} \mathscr{U}_n \subseteq \mathscr{T}(X)$. For the reverse inclusion, let $\bm{x} \in \mathscr{T}(X)$ be arbitrary. By Corollary~\ref{cor:tuple-space-covered-by-sorting-valid-elements}, the family $(\mathscr{V}_\pi)_{\pi \in \mathscr{P}}$ covers $\mathscr{T}(X)$, and since $\varphi : \mathscr{P} \to \mathbb{N}$ is bijective, the set of indices
    \begin{align*}
        I_{\bm{x}} = \big\{k \in \mathbb{N} \;\big|\; \bm{x} \in \mathscr{V}_{\pi_{(k)}}\big\}
    \end{align*}
    is a non-empty subset of $\mathbb{N}$. By the well-ordering principle on $\mathbb{N}$, the set $I_{\bm{x}}$ has a least element $n = \min I_{\bm{x}}$. By definition of $n$, we have $\bm{x} \in \mathscr{V}_{\pi_{(n)}}$ and $\bm{x} \notin \mathscr{V}_{\pi_{(m)}}$ for every $m < n$. Consequently,
    \begin{align*}
        \bm{x} \in \mathscr{V}_{\pi_{(n)}} \setminus \bigcup_{m < n} \mathscr{V}_{\pi_{(m)}} = \mathscr{U}_n \subseteq \bigcup_{k \in \mathbb{N}} \mathscr{U}_k,
    \end{align*}
    which proves that $\mathscr{T}(X) = \bigcup_{n \in \mathbb{N}} \mathscr{U}_n$.

    Next, let $n, k \in \mathbb{N}$ with $n \neq k$, and assume without loss of generality that $m = k < n$. By construction, $\mathscr{U}_k \subseteq \mathscr{V}_{\pi_{(k)}}$, whereas $\mathscr{U}_n$ is disjoint from $\bigcup_{m < n} \mathscr{V}_{\pi_{(m)}}$ and hence disjoint from $\mathscr{V}_{\pi_{(k)}}$. It follows that $\mathscr{U}_n \cap \mathscr{U}_k = \emptyset$, as desired.
\end{proof}

