proof.rawEnvironment:
\begin{proof}
    By Proposition~\ref{prop:disjoint-union-metrisation}, $\Delta$ is indeed a metric on the disjoint union $\mathscr{A}(X)$.

    If $(X,d)$ is complete, then $(\mathscr{T}(X),\mathscr{d}_H)$ is complete by Theorem~\ref{thm:tuple-space-properties}, $(\mathscr{L}(X), d_H^\ast)$ is complete by Theorem~\ref{thm:labelled-properties}, and $(\mathscr{F}(\mathbb{N}), d_H)$ is complete because $(\mathbb{N},|\cdot|)$ is uniformly discrete, hence Proposition~\ref{prop:finite-hausdorff-uniformly-discrete} makes $\mathscr{F}(\mathbb{N})$ uniformly discrete and Proposition~\ref{prop:uniformly-discrete-complete} then yields completeness. Therefore, Proposition~\ref{prop:disjoint-union-metric-properties} implies that $(\mathscr{A}(X),\Delta)$ is complete.

    If $(X,d)$ is separable, then $(\mathscr{T}(X),\mathscr{d}_H)$ is separable by Theorem~\ref{thm:tuple-space-properties}, $(\mathscr{L}(X), d_H^\ast)$ is separable by Theorem~\ref{thm:labelled-properties}, and $(\mathscr{F}(\mathbb{N}), d_H)$ is separable by Proposition~\ref{prop:finite-separable}, since $(\mathbb{N},|\cdot|)$ is countable and hence separable. Another application of Proposition~\ref{prop:disjoint-union-metric-properties} then yields that $(\mathscr{A}(X),\Delta)$ is separable.
\end{proof}

