proof.rawEnvironment:
\begin{proof}
    By the \hyperref[thm:the-standard-machinery]{standard machinery}, for each $n\in\mathbb{N}_0$, the subset of $\mathscr{T}(\mathbb{R})$ consisting of tuples of length $n$ is simultaneously closed and open, and it is homeomorphic to $\mathbb{R}^n$ equipped with its usual topology; for $n=0$, it is the singleton $\{\bm{e}\}$. Taking products, $L$ is closed and open in $\mathscr{T}(\mathbb{R})^k$ and hence Borel, and its subspace topology is the usual topology of $\mathbb{R}^{n_1}\times\dots\times\mathbb{R}^{n_k}$. Therefore, the Borel $\sigma$-algebra of $L$ as a subspace of $\mathscr{T}(\mathbb{R})^k$ coincides with $\mathcal{B}(L)$, and, as $L$ is Borel, a subset of $L$ belongs to $\mathcal{B}(L)$ if and only if it is Borel in $\mathscr{T}(\mathbb{R})^k$. This proves both assertions.
\end{proof}

