\begin{proof}
    We must show that $I_n \in \mathcal{B}(\mathbb{R}^n)$ and $D_n \in \mathcal{B}(\mathbb{R}^n)$ for every $n \in \mathbb{N}$. For $n=1$, we have $I_1 = D_1 = \mathbb{R} \in \mathcal{B}(\mathbb{R})$, so the claim is immediate; assume therefore that $n\geq 2$. First, notice that for each pair of indices $i, j \in \{1, \dots, n\}$, the function
    \begin{align*}
        \Delta_{i,j} :\mathbb{R}^n \to \mathbb{R},
    \end{align*}
    defined by 
    \begin{align*}
        \Delta_{i,j}(\bm{x}) = \bm{x}(i) - \bm{x}(j),
    \end{align*}
    is continuous and hence Borel measurable. Now, notice that 
    \begin{align*}
        I_n = \bigcap_{i =2}^n \Delta_{i, i-1}^{-1}([0,\infty)),
    \end{align*}
    which is a finite intersection of Borel measurable sets and hence Borel measurable. Likewise, 
    \begin{align*}
        D_n = \bigcap_{i=2}^n \Delta_{i, i-1}^{-1}((-\infty,0])
    \end{align*}
    is a finite intersection of Borel measurable sets and hence Borel measurable, as desired. 
\end{proof}

