\begin{rem}[Equivalent point-process measurability]\label{rem:point-process-measurability-equivalence}
    If $(X,d)$ is complete and separable, the Borel $\sigma$-algebra on $(\mathcal{N}(X),\rho)$ is the evaluation $\sigma$-algebra
    \begin{align*}
        \sigma\{\mu\mapsto\mu(B)\mid B\in\mathcal{B}(X)\}.
    \end{align*}
    Consequently, a map $\xi:\Omega\to\mathcal{N}(X)$ is a Borel random element if and only if $\omega\mapsto\xi(\omega)(B)$ is measurable for every $B\in\mathcal{B}(X)$. We use these equivalent random-element and evaluation-wise formulations of a finite point process interchangeably; see \citet{daley2003introduction} and \citet{kallenberg2017random}.
\end{rem}

