\begin{rem}\label{rem:permuted-tuple-notation}
    We adopt a standard abuse of notation, writing $\bm{x}^\pi$ for elements of $\mathscr{T}^\pi(X)$ and dropping the $\pi$-superscript whenever a representative in $\mathscr{T}(X)$ is required. Since $\bm{x} \mapsto \bm{x}^\pi$ is not injective, this tacitly selects a representative; when only a set-theoretic representative is needed, the choice is immaterial, and whenever a representative must depend measurably on a point measure, we instead use Lemma~\ref{lem:measurable-enumeration}.
\end{rem}

