environments.rawEnvironment:
\begin{rem}\label{rem:kernel-quotient-terminology}
    Note that in linear algebra, the kernel refers to the null space of an operator. Here, the kernel equivalence relation groups points mapped to the same value, while $X_\varphi$ denotes the quotient by that relation.
\end{rem}

proof.rawEnvironment:
\begin{proof}
    First, we show that $\Phi$ is well-defined. If $[x]_{\overset{\varphi}{\sim}} = [x']_{\overset{\varphi}{\sim}}$, then $x \overset{\varphi}{\sim} x'$, hence $\varphi(x)=\varphi(x')$. Therefore the value $\Phi([x]_{\overset{\varphi}{\sim}})$ does not depend on the chosen representative.

    We now show injectivity. We must show that 
    \begin{align*}
        \Phi(x_\varphi) = \Phi(x^\prime_\varphi) \implies x_\varphi = x^\prime_\varphi.
    \end{align*}
    Let $x_\varphi, x^\prime_\varphi \in X_\varphi$ be arbitrary. By the definition of $\Phi$, we have that $\Phi(x_\varphi) = \Phi(x^\prime_\varphi)$ implies that $\varphi(x) = \varphi(x^\prime)$, which implies that $x \overset{\varphi}{\sim} x^\prime$, and hence $x_\varphi = x^\prime_\varphi$, as desired.
\end{proof}

