\label{thm:grothendieck-vectorization-is-span}
    Let $X$ be a non-empty set, $V$ a real vector space, and $\varphi : X \to V$ a weakly convex operator. Then, 
    \begin{align*}
        \mathscr{V}(X, \varphi) \cong \mathrm{span}~\varphi(X).
    \end{align*}
    More precisely, the mapping 
    \begin{align*}
        T &: \mathscr{V}(X,\varphi) \to \mathrm{span}~\varphi(X)\\
        [(x_\varphi,z_\varphi)] & \mapsto \Phi(x_\varphi) - \Phi(z_\varphi)
    \end{align*}
    is a linear isomorphism.

proof.rawEnvironment:
\begin{proof}
    Corollary~\ref{thm:grothendieck-vectorization-is-span} makes $T$ a linear isomorphism, and the displayed definition makes it inner-product preserving; this proves the inner-product and isometry claims. An isometry extends uniquely to an isometry of the completions, which proves items 1 and 2. For the map $J(x_\varphi)=[(x_\varphi,0_\varphi)]$, we have $T(J(x_\varphi))=\Phi(x_\varphi)$ because $\Phi(0_\varphi)=0$, so $J$ is isometric. It also preserves addition and multiplication by non-negative scalars, hence its image is a convex cone. Its closure in the Hilbert completion is therefore a non-empty closed convex cone, and Theorem~\ref{thm:closed-convex-cone-closure-projection} supplies the unique metric projection in item 4.
\end{proof}

