\label{defn:positively-matched-state-space-and-price-impact-curve}
    Let $O$ be the order space of \eqref{eq:da-order-space} with price interval $\mathcal{P}=[-500,\overline{p}]$, and let $Q:\mathscr{T}(O)\to[0,\infty)$ and $J_{\bm{m}}(q)\subseteq\mathcal{P}$ be as in Definitions~\ref{defn:excess-supply-and-completed-graph} and~\ref{defn:crossing-price-and-equilibrium-operator}. The \emph{positively matched state space} is the subset
    \begin{align*}
        \mathscr{M}_+=\{\bm{m}\in\mathscr{T}(O)\mid Q(\bm{m})>0\}.
    \end{align*}
    For each $\bm{m}\in\mathscr{M}_+$ with total sell volume $s_*$ and total buy volume $b_*$, the \emph{available-depth interval} is $D_{\bm{m}}=[-b_*,s_*]$, and the \emph{price-impact curve} of $\bm{m}$ is the map
    \begin{align*}
        P_{\bm{m}}:D_{\bm{m}}&\to\mathcal{P}\\
        q&\mapsto\frac{1}{2}\bigl(\min J_{\bm{m}}(q)+\max J_{\bm{m}}(q)\bigr).
    \end{align*}

proof.rawEnvironment:
\begin{proof}
We must show that for every $\bm{m}\in\mathscr{M}_+$, the level set $J_{\bm{m}}(q)$ is a non-empty closed interval for each $q\in D_{\bm{m}}$, so that $P_{\bm{m}}$ is well-defined, and that $P_{\bm{m}}$ satisfies properties \eqref{claim:compact-dom}--\eqref{claim:zero-is-contained}. First, note that because $b_*,s_*\geq0$, the definition $D_{\bm{m}}=[-b_*,s_*]$ immediately yields \eqref{claim:compact-dom} and \eqref{claim:zero-is-contained} once $P_{\bm{m}}$ is shown to be well-defined on $D_{\bm{m}}$.

To show that $J_{\bm{m}}(q)$ is a non-empty closed interval for every $q\in D_{\bm{m}}$, let $x_0<x_1<\dots<x_K$ be the distinct elements of $\{-500,\overline{p}\}$ together with the order prices of $\bm{m}$, so that $x_0=-500$ and $x_K=\overline{p}$. No order price lies in $(x_{k-1},x_k)$, so the volumes offered and bid, and hence $E^{\bm{m}}$, are affine there. The completed graph of $E^{\bm{m}}$ is therefore the union of the segments from $(x_{k-1},E^{\bm{m}}(x_{k-1}+))$ to $(x_k,E^{\bm{m}}(x_k-))$, for $k=1,\dots,K$, and the vertical segments $\{x_k\}\times[E^{\bm{m}}(x_k-),E^{\bm{m}}(x_k+)]$, for $k=0,\dots,K$, where $E^{\bm{m}}(x_0-)=-b_*$ and $E^{\bm{m}}(x_K+)=s_*$. Taken in their order along the graph, consecutive segments share an endpoint, and their ranges of levels are closed intervals that cover $[-b_*,s_*]$ and overlap only at their endpoints. Consequently, $J_{\bm{m}}(q)$ is non-empty for every $q\in D_{\bm{m}}$, and it is closed because the completed graph is a finite union of closed segments.

Next, since $E^{\bm{m}}$ is non-decreasing, $p<p^\prime$ implies $E^{\bm{m}}(p+)\leq E^{\bm{m}}(p^\prime-)$. If $p<p^{\prime\prime}<p^\prime$ with $p,p^\prime\in J_{\bm{m}}(q)$, then
\begin{align*}
    E^{\bm{m}}(p^{\prime\prime}-)\leq E^{\bm{m}}(p^\prime-)\leq q\leq E^{\bm{m}}(p+)\leq E^{\bm{m}}(p^{\prime\prime}+),
\end{align*}
so $p^{\prime\prime}\in J_{\bm{m}}(q)$. Thus $J_{\bm{m}}(q)$ is a non-empty closed interval, and $P_{\bm{m}}$ is well-defined on $D_{\bm{m}}$.

To establish \eqref{claim:increasing}, let $q,q^\prime\in D_{\bm{m}}$ satisfy $q<q^\prime$, and let $p\in J_{\bm{m}}(q)$ and $p^\prime\in J_{\bm{m}}(q^\prime)$. Then $p\leq p^\prime$, because $p^\prime<p$ would yield $q^\prime\leq E^{\bm{m}}(p^\prime+)\leq E^{\bm{m}}(p-)\leq q$, a contradiction. Taking the maximum over $p\in J_{\bm{m}}(q)$ and the minimum over $p^\prime\in J_{\bm{m}}(q^\prime)$ gives
\begin{align*}
    P_{\bm{m}}(q)\leq\max J_{\bm{m}}(q)\leq\min J_{\bm{m}}(q^\prime)\leq P_{\bm{m}}(q^\prime),
\end{align*}
which proves that $P_{\bm{m}}$ is increasing.

Finally, to establish \eqref{claim:pw-affine}, we remove from $D_{\bm{m}}$ the finitely many levels $E^{\bm{m}}(x_k\pm)$. Every remaining level $q$ lies in the interior of the range of levels of exactly one segment, and no other segment reaches $q$, since the endpoints of these ranges have been removed. If the segment is the $k$th non-vertical one, then $E^{\bm{m}}$ is affine with positive slope on $(x_{k-1},x_k)$, the set $J_{\bm{m}}(q)$ consists of the single price at which $E^{\bm{m}}$ takes the value $q$, and $P_{\bm{m}}$ agrees with the affine inverse of $E^{\bm{m}}$ on the interior of the range. If it is the vertical segment at $x_k$, then $J_{\bm{m}}(q)=\{x_k\}$, and $P_{\bm{m}}$ equals $x_k$ on the interior of the range. Hence $D_{\bm{m}}$ is the disjoint union of finitely many open intervals, on each of which $P_{\bm{m}}$ is affine, and finitely many singletons, which establishes \eqref{claim:pw-affine}, as desired.
\end{proof}

