proof.rawEnvironment:
\begin{proof}
    We must show that for all $f,g\in\mathpzc{P}$ and all $\alpha,\beta\geq0$, there exists $h\in\mathpzc{P}$ such that $\alpha\overline{\Lambda}(f)+\beta\overline{\Lambda}(g)=\overline{\Lambda}(h)$. Let $f,g\in\mathpzc{P}$ and $\alpha,\beta\geq0$ be given. By Proposition~\ref{prop:linear-parametrisation-convex}, there exists $h\in\mathpzc{P}$ such that $\alpha\Lambda(f)+\beta\Lambda(g)=\Lambda(h)$ pointwise in $\mathcal{L}^2([-1,1];\mathbb{R}^2)$. Since the quotient map $z\mapsto[z]$ from $\mathcal{L}^2([-1,1];\mathbb{R}^2)$ to $L^2([-1,1];\mathbb{R}^2)$ is linear, passing to equivalence classes gives
    \begin{align*}
        \alpha\overline{\Lambda}(f)+\beta\overline{\Lambda}(g)
        =\bigl[\alpha\Lambda(f)+\beta\Lambda(g)\bigr]
        =\bigl[\Lambda(h)\bigr]
        =\overline{\Lambda}(h),
    \end{align*}
    which proves that $\overline{\Lambda}$ is weakly convex, as desired.
\end{proof}

