environments.rawEnvironment:
\begin{cor}[The Sided Parametrisation Is Not Weakly Convex]\label{cor:sided-parametrisation-not-weakly-convex}
    Let $\Psi:\mathpzc{P}_{\mathrm{s}}\to\mathcal{L}^2([-1,1];\mathbb{R}^2)$ and $\overline{\Psi}:\mathpzc{P}_{\mathrm{s}}\to L^2([-1,1];\mathbb{R}^2)$ be the sided parametrisation operators of Definition~\ref{defn:parametrisation-operator} on the set $\mathpzc{P}_{\mathrm{s}}\subseteq\mathpzc{P}$ of simple price curves. Neither $\Psi$ nor $\overline{\Psi}$ is weakly convex; equivalently, neither $\Psi(\mathpzc{P}_{\mathrm{s}})$ nor $\overline{\Psi}(\mathpzc{P}_{\mathrm{s}})$ is a convex cone.
\end{cor}

proof.rawEnvironment:
\begin{proof}
We must show first that the maps $Z:\mathscr{L}\to\allowbreak L^2([-1,1];\mathbb{R}^2)$ and $\bm{m}\mapsto P_{\bm{m}}(0)$ are Borel measurable with respect to $\mathcal{B}(\mathscr{L})$, and second that $\mathbb{E}\bigl[\lVert Z(\bm{M})\rVert_{L^2([-1,1];\mathbb{R}^2)}^2\bigr]<\infty$ whenever $\mathbb{E}[D(\bm{M})^2]<\infty$.

To establish Borel measurability, we decompose each fixed-length layer of $\mathscr{L}$ into a finite Borel partition on whose cells every stage of the construction coincides with a fixed continuous map; Theorem~\ref{thm:measurable-partition} then yields global Borel measurability. By the \hyperref[thm:the-standard-machinery]{standard machinery} (Theorem~\ref{thm:the-standard-machinery}), the fixed-length layers
\begin{align*}
    \mathscr{L}_{n,n^\prime}=\mathscr{L}\cap\bigl((\mathbb{R}\times[0,\infty))^{n}\times(\mathbb{R}\times[0,\infty))^{n^\prime}\bigr),\qquad n,n^\prime\in\mathbb{N},
\end{align*}
form a countable Borel partition of $\mathscr{L}$ in its subspace topology, because the fixed-length product layers are clopen and hence Borel in the ambient product of tuple spaces. Since every comparison set below is defined relative to $\mathscr{L}_{n,n^\prime}$ and therefore belongs to the subspace Borel $\sigma$-algebra $\mathcal{B}(\mathscr{L}_{n,n^\prime})$, it suffices to fix $(n,n^\prime)\in\mathbb{N}^2$ and to construct a finite Borel partition of $\mathscr{L}_{n,n^\prime}$ on each cell of which $Z$ and $\bm{m}\mapsto P_{\bm{m}}(0)$ are continuous.

First, we partition $\mathscr{L}_{n,n^\prime}$ according to the sorting permutations of the sell and buy lists. The sell list $\bm{s}$ is sorted by increasing price and, within a price, by increasing volume, while the buy list $\bm{b}$ is sorted by decreasing price and, within a price, by increasing volume. Both relations are total orders on $\mathbb{R}\times[0,\infty)$, and both are Borel in the sense of Section~\ref{sec:sorting-properties} because their graphs are Borel subsets of $(\mathbb{R}\times[0,\infty))^2$. Let $\varsigma_{\mathrm{sell}}$ and $\varsigma_{\mathrm{buy}}$ denote the corresponding minimal sorting operators of Theorem~\ref{thm:minimal-sorting-measurable}. By Proposition~\ref{prop:measurable-partition-sorting} and Corollary~\ref{cor:local-continuity-sorting}, for each pair of permutations $(\pi_s,\pi_b)\in\mathcal{P}_n\times\mathcal{P}_{n^\prime}$, the set
\begin{align*}
    \mathscr{S}_{\pi_s,\pi_b}
    =\bigl\{\bm{m}=(\bm{s},\bm{b})\in\mathscr{L}_{n,n^\prime}\bigm| \varsigma_{\mathrm{sell}}(\bm{s})=\bm{s}[\pi_s]\;\land\;\varsigma_{\mathrm{buy}}(\bm{b})=\bm{b}[\pi_b]\bigr\}
\end{align*}
is Borel measurable in $\mathscr{L}_{n,n^\prime}$, and on $\mathscr{S}_{\pi_s,\pi_b}$ the sorting maps coincide with the continuous coordinate permutations $\bm{s}\mapsto\bm{s}[\pi_s]$ and $\bm{b}\mapsto\bm{b}[\pi_b]$. Because distinct permutations can produce the same sorted tuple when entries coincide, we fix a total ordering of the finite product $\mathcal{P}_n\times\mathcal{P}_{n^\prime}$ and replace each $\mathscr{S}_{\pi_s,\pi_b}$ by its set difference from the union of all preceding cells; retaining the notation $\mathscr{S}_{\pi_s,\pi_b}$ for these disjoint Borel sets yields a finite Borel partition of $\mathscr{L}_{n,n^\prime}$ on each cell of which the same continuous sorting formula holds. Furthermore, because the pointwise maximum of finitely many continuous functions is continuous, the running maxima of the sorted volumes depend continuously on $\bm{m}\in\mathscr{S}_{\pi_s,\pi_b}$.

Second, to formalise the dependence of the excess supply on the order prices, let $N_p=n+n^\prime+2$ and define the combined price-extraction map $\bm{\pi}:\mathscr{L}_{n,n^\prime}\to\mathcal{P}^{N_p}$ by concatenating the lower price limit, the sorted sell prices, the sorted buy prices, and the upper price limit:
\begin{align*}
    \bm{\pi}(\bm{s},\bm{b})=(-500)\oplus\bm{P}_{\mathrm{price}}\bigl(\varsigma_{\mathrm{sell}}(\bm{s})\bigr)\oplus\bm{P}_{\mathrm{price}}\bigl(\varsigma_{\mathrm{buy}}(\bm{b})\bigr)\oplus(\overline{p}).
\end{align*}
Since $\varsigma_{\mathrm{sell}}$ and $\varsigma_{\mathrm{buy}}$ are continuous on each cell $\mathscr{S}_{\pi_s,\pi_b}$, and coordinate projections and concatenation are continuous by Proposition~\ref{prop:tuple-projection-continuous} and Theorem~\ref{thm:analytic-properties-actions}, the map $\bm{\pi}$ is continuous on $\mathscr{S}_{\pi_s,\pi_b}$. For every pair of indices $u,v\in\{1,\dots,N_p\}$, define the elementary comparison sets
\begin{align*}
    E_{u,v}&=\bigl\{\bm{m}\in\mathscr{L}_{n,n^\prime}\bigm|\bm{\pi}(\bm{m})(u)=\bm{\pi}(\bm{m})(v)\bigr\},\\
    L_{u,v}&=\bigl\{\bm{m}\in\mathscr{L}_{n,n^\prime}\bigm|\bm{\pi}(\bm{m})(u)<\bm{\pi}(\bm{m})(v)\bigr\}.
\end{align*}
Because the diagonal is closed in $\mathbb{R}^2$ and the strict order relation is open in $\mathbb{R}^2$, each $E_{u,v}$ and each $L_{u,v}$ has a Borel intersection with $\mathscr{S}_{\pi_s,\pi_b}$. Any total preorder on the finite index set $\{1,\dots,N_p\}$ is specified by an ordered partition $\mathcal{C}=(C_1,\dots,C_r)$ of $\{1,\dots,N_p\}$ into non-empty equivalence classes. For each such ordered partition $\mathcal{C}$, define the cell
\begin{align*}
    \mathscr{W}(\mathcal{C})=\left(\bigcap_{k=1}^r\bigcap_{u,v\in C_k}E_{u,v}\right)\cap\left(\bigcap_{1\leq k<l\leq r}\bigcap_{u\in C_k,\,v\in C_l}L_{u,v}\right).
\end{align*}
As $\mathcal{C}$ ranges over the finitely many ordered partitions of $\{1,\dots,N_p\}$, the sets $\mathscr{S}_{\pi_s,\pi_b}\cap\mathscr{W}(\mathcal{C})$ form a finite Borel refinement of $\mathscr{S}_{\pi_s,\pi_b}$. On each non-empty cell of this refinement, the number $r$ of distinct prices is constant, and choosing the minimum index $u_k=\min C_k$ in each equivalence class yields the strictly increasing distinct price coordinates
\begin{align*}
    x_k(\bm{m})=\bm{\pi}(\bm{m})(u_k),\qquad k\in\{1,\dots,r\},
\end{align*}
which satisfy $-500=x_1(\bm{m})<x_2(\bm{m})<\dots<x_r(\bm{m})=\overline{p}$ and depend continuously on $\bm{m}$. Furthermore, on each such cell, the position of $x_k(\bm{m})$ relative to the sorted sell and buy prices is fixed, so the one-sided limits at $x_k(\bm{m})$ of the offered and bid volumes are given by fixed expressions, namely $0$, a running maximum of the sorted volumes, or the linear interpolation at $x_k(\bm{m})$ between two consecutive list entries whose price difference is strictly positive on the cell. Consequently, the maps $\bm{m}\mapsto E^{\bm{m}}(x_k(\bm{m})-)$ and $\bm{m}\mapsto E^{\bm{m}}(x_k(\bm{m})+)$ are continuous on the cell for every $k\in\{1,\dots,r\}$.

Third, we refine each cell according to the jump structure of $E^{\bm{m}}$ and the location of zero quantity. For each $k\in\{1,\dots,r\}$, define the Borel set
\begin{align*}
    J_k=\bigl\{\bm{m}\in\mathscr{L}_{n,n^\prime}\bigm| E^{\bm{m}}(x_k(\bm{m})-)=E^{\bm{m}}(x_k(\bm{m})+)\bigr\},
\end{align*}
and for each subset $K\subseteq\{1,\dots,r\}$, set
\begin{align*}
    \mathscr{J}(K)=\left(\bigcap_{k\in K}J_k\right)\cap\left(\bigcap_{k\in\{1,\dots,r\}\setminus K}J_k^{\mathrm{c}}\right).
\end{align*}
Intersecting the previous cells with $\mathscr{J}(K)$ fixes the number $N=2r-\lvert K\rvert$ of distinct vertices of the completed graph of $E^{\bm{m}}$. Interchanging the price and excess-supply coordinates then yields a continuous vertex path $\bm{m}\mapsto\bigl((q_1(\bm{m}),p_1(\bm{m})),\allowbreak\dots,\allowbreak(q_N(\bm{m}),p_N(\bm{m}))\bigr)$ of $P_{\bm{m}}$, running from $(-b_*,x_1)$ to $(s_*,\overline{p})$ with $q_1(\bm{m})\leq\dots\leq q_N(\bm{m})$. To extract the crossing price and the one-sided limits at zero, define for each $i\in\{1,\dots,N\}$ the sign sets
\begin{align*}
    Z_i^-=\bigl\{\bm{m}\bigm| q_i(\bm{m})<0\bigr\},\qquad
    Z_i^0=\bigl\{\bm{m}\bigm| q_i(\bm{m})=0\bigr\},\qquad
    Z_i^+=\bigl\{\bm{m}\bigm| q_i(\bm{m})>0\bigr\}.
\end{align*}
For every sign assignment $\bm{\sigma}=(\sigma_1,\dots,\sigma_N)\in\{-,0,+\}^N$, the intersection $\mathscr{Z}(\bm{\sigma})=\bigcap_{i=1}^N Z_i^{\sigma_i}$ is Borel measurable. On each non-empty cell intersected with $\mathscr{Z}(\bm{\sigma})$, the index sets $I_-=\{i\mid\sigma_i=-\}$, $I_0=\{i\mid\sigma_i=0\}$, and $I_+=\{i\mid\sigma_i=+\}$ are fixed. If $I_0\neq\emptyset$ with $i_{\min}=\min I_0$ and $i_{\max}=\max I_0$, then $P_{\bm{m}}(0)=\frac{1}{2}(p_{i_{\min}}(\bm{m})+p_{i_{\max}}(\bm{m}))$, $P_{\bm{m}}(0-)=p_{i_{\min}}(\bm{m})$, and $P_{\bm{m}}(0+)=p_{i_{\max}}(\bm{m})$. If $I_0=\emptyset$, then there is a unique index $i_*\in I_-$ such that $i_*+1\in I_+$, and $P_{\bm{m}}(0)=P_{\bm{m}}(0-)=P_{\bm{m}}(0+)$ coincides with the linear interpolation of $p_{i_*}(\bm{m})$ and $p_{i_*+1}(\bm{m})$ at $0$, whose denominator $q_{i_*+1}(\bm{m})-q_{i_*}(\bm{m})$ is strictly positive on the cell. In either case, $P_{\bm{m}}(0)$, $P_{\bm{m}}(0-)$, and $P_{\bm{m}}(0+)$ depend continuously on $\bm{m}$.

Fourth, we partition each resulting cell to fix the breakpoints of $P_{\bm{m}}$ on the positive and negative sides. We write out the positive side; the negative side is partitioned identically after replacing $q$ with $-q$. On each cell of the current refinement, the positive-side vertex path $(q_1^+(\bm{m}),p_1^+(\bm{m})),\dots,(q_{n_+}^+(\bm{m}),p_{n_+}^+(\bm{m}))$, starting at $(0,P_{\bm{m}}(0+))$ and continuing through the vertices with $i\in I_+$, has a fixed length $n_+$ and depends continuously on $\bm{m}$. For each $k\in\{1,\dots,n_+-1\}$, define the equality sets
\begin{align*}
    Q_k^+=\bigl\{\bm{m}\bigm| q_k^+(\bm{m})=q_{k+1}^+(\bm{m})\bigr\},\qquad
    P_k^+=\bigl\{\bm{m}\bigm| p_k^+(\bm{m})=p_{k+1}^+(\bm{m})\bigr\}.
\end{align*}
Intersecting the current cell with each combination of $Q_k^+,(Q_k^+)^{\mathrm{c}}$ and $P_k^+,(P_k^+)^{\mathrm{c}}$ over $k\in\{1,\dots,n_+-1\}$ yields a finite Borel refinement on which the indices of repeated vertices, vertical segments, and segments of positive width are constant. After omitting repeated vertices, let $(\tilde{q}_1^+(\bm{m}),\tilde{p}_1^+(\bm{m})),\allowbreak\dots,\allowbreak(\tilde{q}_{\tilde{n}_+}^+(\bm{m}),\tilde{p}_{\tilde{n}_+}^+(\bm{m}))$ denote the reduced positive-side vertex path. For each interior index $k\in\{2,\dots,\tilde{n}_+-1\}$ that joins two segments of positive width, define the collinearity set
\begin{align*}
    A_k^+=\bigl\{\bm{m}\bigm|\bigl(\tilde{q}_k^+(\bm{m})-\tilde{q}_{k-1}^+(\bm{m})\bigr)\bigl(\tilde{p}_{k+1}^+(\bm{m})-\tilde{p}_k^+(\bm{m})\bigr)\\
    =\bigl(\tilde{p}_k^+(\bm{m})-\tilde{p}_{k-1}^+(\bm{m})\bigr)\bigl(\tilde{q}_{k+1}^+(\bm{m})-\tilde{q}_k^+(\bm{m})\bigr)\bigr\}.
\end{align*}
Because $\tilde{q}_k^+$ and $\tilde{p}_k^+$ are continuous on the current cell, each $A_k^+$ is closed in the cell and hence Borel measurable. Intersecting with $\bigl(\bigcap_{k\in B}A_k^+\bigr)\cap\bigl(\bigcap_{k\notin B}(A_k^+)^{\mathrm{c}}\bigr)$ for each subset $B$ of such interior indices fixes the positive-side breakpoints, the number $U$ of pieces of positive width on the positive side, and the endpoints of every piece as fixed continuous vertex coordinates of the path. Performing the analogous finite intersection on the negative side fixes the number $L$ of negative-side pieces of positive width and their continuous endpoints.

Finally, on each cell of the combined finite Borel partition of $\mathscr{L}_{n,n^\prime}$, the numbers $L$ and $U$ are constant, and $\Psi(P_{\bm{m}})$ linearly interpolates the endpoints of the $j$th positive-width piece over $[(j-1)/U,j/U]$ on the positive side and over $[j/L,(j+1)/L]$ on the negative side. Recording the quantity endpoints and the one-sided price endpoints of every piece (retaining both price endpoints at a jump) yields a continuous finite-dimensional vector of endpoint coordinates on the cell. Because the map from this finite-dimensional vector of endpoint coordinates to the equivalence class of the corresponding piecewise-affine interpolant in $L^2([-1,1];\mathbb{R}^2)$ is linear and continuous, both $Z$ and $\bm{m}\mapsto P_{\bm{m}}(0)$ are continuous on every cell of the partition. Since the countable union of these finite partitions over $(n,n^\prime)\in\mathbb{N}^2$ forms a countable Borel partition of $\mathscr{L}$, Theorem~\ref{thm:measurable-partition} implies that $Z$ and $\bm{m}\mapsto P_{\bm{m}}(0)$ are Borel measurable on $\mathscr{L}$.

To prove the second claim, let $\bm{M}$ be a measurable random state in $\mathscr{L}$ with $\mathbb{E}[D(\bm{M})^2]<\infty$, and set $M_p=\max(500,\overline{p})$. Since every point of $\Psi(P_{\bm{M}})(u)$ lies on the completed graph of $P_{\bm{M}}$, we have $\lvert\psi_{P_{\bm{M}}}(u)\rvert\leq D(\bm{M})$ and $\lvert P_{\bm{M}}(\psi_{P_{\bm{M}}}(u))\rvert\leq M_p$ for all $u\in[-1,1]$. Integrating over $u\in[-1,1]$ yields
\begin{align*}
    \lVert Z(\bm{M})\rVert_{L^2([-1,1];\mathbb{R}^2)}^2
    =\int_{-1}^1\Bigl(\bigl\lvert\psi_{P_{\bm{M}}}(u)\bigr\rvert^2+\bigl\lvert P_{\bm{M}}\bigl(\psi_{P_{\bm{M}}}(u)\bigr)\bigr\rvert^2\Bigr)\,\mathrm{d}u
    \leq 2D(\bm{M})^2+2M_p^2.
\end{align*}
Taking expectations gives $\mathbb{E}[\lVert Z(\bm{M})\rVert_{L^2([-1,1];\mathbb{R}^2)}^2]\leq 2\mathbb{E}[D(\bm{M})^2]+2M_p^2<\infty$. Because $L^2([-1,1];\mathbb{R}^2)$ is separable, Borel measurability of $Z(\bm{M})$ implies strong measurability by Pettis's measurability theorem, so $Z(\bm{M})$ is a square-integrable Hilbert-valued random element, as desired.
\end{proof}

proof.text:
We must show first that the maps $Z:\mathscr{L}\to\allowbreak L^2([-1,1];\mathbb{R}^2)$ and $\bm{m}\mapsto P_{\bm{m}}(0)$ are Borel measurable with respect to $\mathcal{B}(\mathscr{L})$, and second that $\mathbb{E}\bigl[\lVert Z(\bm{M})\rVert_{L^2([-1,1];\mathbb{R}^2)}^2\bigr]<\infty$ whenever $\mathbb{E}[D(\bm{M})^2]<\infty$.

To establish Borel measurability, we decompose each fixed-length layer of $\mathscr{L}$ into a finite Borel partition on whose cells every stage of the construction coincides with a fixed continuous map; Theorem~\ref{thm:measurable-partition} then yields global Borel measurability. By the \hyperref[thm:the-standard-machinery]{standard machinery} (Theorem~\ref{thm:the-standard-machinery}), the fixed-length layers
\begin{align*}
    \mathscr{L}_{n,n^\prime}=\mathscr{L}\cap\bigl((\mathbb{R}\times[0,\infty))^{n}\times(\mathbb{R}\times[0,\infty))^{n^\prime}\bigr),\qquad n,n^\prime\in\mathbb{N},
\end{align*}
form a countable Borel partition of $\mathscr{L}$ in its subspace topology, because the fixed-length product layers are clopen and hence Borel in the ambient product of tuple spaces. Since every comparison set below is defined relative to $\mathscr{L}_{n,n^\prime}$ and therefore belongs to the subspace Borel $\sigma$-algebra $\mathcal{B}(\mathscr{L}_{n,n^\prime})$, it suffices to fix $(n,n^\prime)\in\mathbb{N}^2$ and to construct a finite Borel partition of $\mathscr{L}_{n,n^\prime}$ on each cell of which $Z$ and $\bm{m}\mapsto P_{\bm{m}}(0)$ are continuous.

First, we partition $\mathscr{L}_{n,n^\prime}$ according to the sorting permutations of the sell and buy lists. The sell list $\bm{s}$ is sorted by increasing price and, within a price, by increasing volume, while the buy list $\bm{b}$ is sorted by decreasing price and, within a price, by increasing volume. Both relations are total orders on $\mathbb{R}\times[0,\infty)$, and both are Borel in the sense of Section~\ref{sec:sorting-properties} because their graphs are Borel subsets of $(\mathbb{R}\times[0,\infty))^2$. Let $\varsigma_{\mathrm{sell}}$ and $\varsigma_{\mathrm{buy}}$ denote the corresponding minimal sorting operators of Theorem~\ref{thm:minimal-sorting-measurable}. By Proposition~\ref{prop:measurable-partition-sorting} and Corollary~\ref{cor:local-continuity-sorting}, for each pair of permutations $(\pi_s,\pi_b)\in\mathcal{P}_n\times\mathcal{P}_{n^\prime}$, the set
\begin{align*}
    \mathscr{S}_{\pi_s,\pi_b}
    =\bigl\{\bm{m}=(\bm{s},\bm{b})\in\mathscr{L}_{n,n^\prime}\bigm| \varsigma_{\mathrm{sell}}(\bm{s})=\bm{s}[\pi_s]\;\land\;\varsigma_{\mathrm{buy}}(\bm{b})=\bm{b}[\pi_b]\bigr\}
\end{align*}
is Borel measurable in $\mathscr{L}_{n,n^\prime}$, and on $\mathscr{S}_{\pi_s,\pi_b}$ the sorting maps coincide with the continuous coordinate permutations $\bm{s}\mapsto\bm{s}[\pi_s]$ and $\bm{b}\mapsto\bm{b}[\pi_b]$. Because distinct permutations can produce the same sorted tuple when entries coincide, we fix a total ordering of the finite product $\mathcal{P}_n\times\mathcal{P}_{n^\prime}$ and replace each $\mathscr{S}_{\pi_s,\pi_b}$ by its set difference from the union of all preceding cells; retaining the notation $\mathscr{S}_{\pi_s,\pi_b}$ for these disjoint Borel sets yields a finite Borel partition of $\mathscr{L}_{n,n^\prime}$ on each cell of which the same continuous sorting formula holds. Furthermore, because the pointwise maximum of finitely many continuous functions is continuous, the running maxima of the sorted volumes depend continuously on $\bm{m}\in\mathscr{S}_{\pi_s,\pi_b}$.

Second, to formalise the dependence of the excess supply on the order prices, let $N_p=n+n^\prime+2$ and define the combined price-extraction map $\bm{\pi}:\mathscr{L}_{n,n^\prime}\to\mathcal{P}^{N_p}$ by concatenating the lower price limit, the sorted sell prices, the sorted buy prices, and the upper price limit:
\begin{align*}
    \bm{\pi}(\bm{s},\bm{b})=(-500)\oplus\bm{P}_{\mathrm{price}}\bigl(\varsigma_{\mathrm{sell}}(\bm{s})\bigr)\oplus\bm{P}_{\mathrm{price}}\bigl(\varsigma_{\mathrm{buy}}(\bm{b})\bigr)\oplus(\overline{p}).
\end{align*}
Since $\varsigma_{\mathrm{sell}}$ and $\varsigma_{\mathrm{buy}}$ are continuous on each cell $\mathscr{S}_{\pi_s,\pi_b}$, and coordinate projections and concatenation are continuous by Proposition~\ref{prop:tuple-projection-continuous} and Theorem~\ref{thm:analytic-properties-actions}, the map $\bm{\pi}$ is continuous on $\mathscr{S}_{\pi_s,\pi_b}$. For every pair of indices $u,v\in\{1,\dots,N_p\}$, define the elementary comparison sets
\begin{align*}
    E_{u,v}&=\bigl\{\bm{m}\in\mathscr{L}_{n,n^\prime}\bigm|\bm{\pi}(\bm{m})(u)=\bm{\pi}(\bm{m})(v)\bigr\},\\
    L_{u,v}&=\bigl\{\bm{m}\in\mathscr{L}_{n,n^\prime}\bigm|\bm{\pi}(\bm{m})(u)<\bm{\pi}(\bm{m})(v)\bigr\}.
\end{align*}
Because the diagonal is closed in $\mathbb{R}^2$ and the strict order relation is open in $\mathbb{R}^2$, each $E_{u,v}$ and each $L_{u,v}$ has a Borel intersection with $\mathscr{S}_{\pi_s,\pi_b}$. Any total preorder on the finite index set $\{1,\dots,N_p\}$ is specified by an ordered partition $\mathcal{C}=(C_1,\dots,C_r)$ of $\{1,\dots,N_p\}$ into non-empty equivalence classes. For each such ordered partition $\mathcal{C}$, define the cell
\begin{align*}
    \mathscr{W}(\mathcal{C})=\left(\bigcap_{k=1}^r\bigcap_{u,v\in C_k}E_{u,v}\right)\cap\left(\bigcap_{1\leq k<l\leq r}\bigcap_{u\in C_k,\,v\in C_l}L_{u,v}\right).
\end{align*}
As $\mathcal{C}$ ranges over the finitely many ordered partitions of $\{1,\dots,N_p\}$, the sets $\mathscr{S}_{\pi_s,\pi_b}\cap\mathscr{W}(\mathcal{C})$ form a finite Borel refinement of $\mathscr{S}_{\pi_s,\pi_b}$. On each non-empty cell of this refinement, the number $r$ of distinct prices is constant, and choosing the minimum index $u_k=\min C_k$ in each equivalence class yields the strictly increasing distinct price coordinates
\begin{align*}
    x_k(\bm{m})=\bm{\pi}(\bm{m})(u_k),\qquad k\in\{1,\dots,r\},
\end{align*}
which satisfy $-500=x_1(\bm{m})<x_2(\bm{m})<\dots<x_r(\bm{m})=\overline{p}$ and depend continuously on $\bm{m}$. Furthermore, on each such cell, the position of $x_k(\bm{m})$ relative to the sorted sell and buy prices is fixed, so the one-sided limits at $x_k(\bm{m})$ of the offered and bid volumes are given by fixed expressions, namely $0$, a running maximum of the sorted volumes, or the linear interpolation at $x_k(\bm{m})$ between two consecutive list entries whose price difference is strictly positive on the cell. Consequently, the maps $\bm{m}\mapsto E^{\bm{m}}(x_k(\bm{m})-)$ and $\bm{m}\mapsto E^{\bm{m}}(x_k(\bm{m})+)$ are continuous on the cell for every $k\in\{1,\dots,r\}$.

Third, we refine each cell according to the jump structure of $E^{\bm{m}}$ and the location of zero quantity. For each $k\in\{1,\dots,r\}$, define the Borel set
\begin{align*}
    J_k=\bigl\{\bm{m}\in\mathscr{L}_{n,n^\prime}\bigm| E^{\bm{m}}(x_k(\bm{m})-)=E^{\bm{m}}(x_k(\bm{m})+)\bigr\},
\end{align*}
and for each subset $K\subseteq\{1,\dots,r\}$, set
\begin{align*}
    \mathscr{J}(K)=\left(\bigcap_{k\in K}J_k\right)\cap\left(\bigcap_{k\in\{1,\dots,r\}\setminus K}J_k^{\mathrm{c}}\right).
\end{align*}
Intersecting the previous cells with $\mathscr{J}(K)$ fixes the number $N=2r-\lvert K\rvert$ of distinct vertices of the completed graph of $E^{\bm{m}}$. Interchanging the price and excess-supply coordinates then yields a continuous vertex path $\bm{m}\mapsto\bigl((q_1(\bm{m}),p_1(\bm{m})),\allowbreak\dots,\allowbreak(q_N(\bm{m}),p_N(\bm{m}))\bigr)$ of $P_{\bm{m}}$, running from $(-b_*,x_1)$ to $(s_*,\overline{p})$ with $q_1(\bm{m})\leq\dots\leq q_N(\bm{m})$. To extract the crossing price and the one-sided limits at zero, define for each $i\in\{1,\dots,N\}$ the sign sets
\begin{align*}
    Z_i^-=\bigl\{\bm{m}\bigm| q_i(\bm{m})<0\bigr\},\qquad
    Z_i^0=\bigl\{\bm{m}\bigm| q_i(\bm{m})=0\bigr\},\qquad
    Z_i^+=\bigl\{\bm{m}\bigm| q_i(\bm{m})>0\bigr\}.
\end{align*}
For every sign assignment $\bm{\sigma}=(\sigma_1,\dots,\sigma_N)\in\{-,0,+\}^N$, the intersection $\mathscr{Z}(\bm{\sigma})=\bigcap_{i=1}^N Z_i^{\sigma_i}$ is Borel measurable. On each non-empty cell intersected with $\mathscr{Z}(\bm{\sigma})$, the index sets $I_-=\{i\mid\sigma_i=-\}$, $I_0=\{i\mid\sigma_i=0\}$, and $I_+=\{i\mid\sigma_i=+\}$ are fixed. If $I_0\neq\emptyset$ with $i_{\min}=\min I_0$ and $i_{\max}=\max I_0$, then $P_{\bm{m}}(0)=\frac{1}{2}(p_{i_{\min}}(\bm{m})+p_{i_{\max}}(\bm{m}))$, $P_{\bm{m}}(0-)=p_{i_{\min}}(\bm{m})$, and $P_{\bm{m}}(0+)=p_{i_{\max}}(\bm{m})$. If $I_0=\emptyset$, then there is a unique index $i_*\in I_-$ such that $i_*+1\in I_+$, and $P_{\bm{m}}(0)=P_{\bm{m}}(0-)=P_{\bm{m}}(0+)$ coincides with the linear interpolation of $p_{i_*}(\bm{m})$ and $p_{i_*+1}(\bm{m})$ at $0$, whose denominator $q_{i_*+1}(\bm{m})-q_{i_*}(\bm{m})$ is strictly positive on the cell. In either case, $P_{\bm{m}}(0)$, $P_{\bm{m}}(0-)$, and $P_{\bm{m}}(0+)$ depend continuously on $\bm{m}$.

Fourth, we partition each resulting cell to fix the breakpoints of $P_{\bm{m}}$ on the positive and negative sides. We write out the positive side; the negative side is partitioned identically after replacing $q$ with $-q$. On each cell of the current refinement, the positive-side vertex path $(q_1^+(\bm{m}),p_1^+(\bm{m})),\dots,(q_{n_+}^+(\bm{m}),p_{n_+}^+(\bm{m}))$, starting at $(0,P_{\bm{m}}(0+))$ and continuing through the vertices with $i\in I_+$, has a fixed length $n_+$ and depends continuously on $\bm{m}$. For each $k\in\{1,\dots,n_+-1\}$, define the equality sets
\begin{align*}
    Q_k^+=\bigl\{\bm{m}\bigm| q_k^+(\bm{m})=q_{k+1}^+(\bm{m})\bigr\},\qquad
    P_k^+=\bigl\{\bm{m}\bigm| p_k^+(\bm{m})=p_{k+1}^+(\bm{m})\bigr\}.
\end{align*}
Intersecting the current cell with each combination of $Q_k^+,(Q_k^+)^{\mathrm{c}}$ and $P_k^+,(P_k^+)^{\mathrm{c}}$ over $k\in\{1,\dots,n_+-1\}$ yields a finite Borel refinement on which the indices of repeated vertices, vertical segments, and segments of positive width are constant. After omitting repeated vertices, let $(\tilde{q}_1^+(\bm{m}),\tilde{p}_1^+(\bm{m})),\allowbreak\dots,\allowbreak(\tilde{q}_{\tilde{n}_+}^+(\bm{m}),\tilde{p}_{\tilde{n}_+}^+(\bm{m}))$ denote the reduced positive-side vertex path. For each interior index $k\in\{2,\dots,\tilde{n}_+-1\}$ that joins two segments of positive width, define the collinearity set
\begin{align*}
    A_k^+=\bigl\{\bm{m}\bigm|\bigl(\tilde{q}_k^+(\bm{m})-\tilde{q}_{k-1}^+(\bm{m})\bigr)\bigl(\tilde{p}_{k+1}^+(\bm{m})-\tilde{p}_k^+(\bm{m})\bigr)\\
    =\bigl(\tilde{p}_k^+(\bm{m})-\tilde{p}_{k-1}^+(\bm{m})\bigr)\bigl(\tilde{q}_{k+1}^+(\bm{m})-\tilde{q}_k^+(\bm{m})\bigr)\bigr\}.
\end{align*}
Because $\tilde{q}_k^+$ and $\tilde{p}_k^+$ are continuous on the current cell, each $A_k^+$ is closed in the cell and hence Borel measurable. Intersecting with $\bigl(\bigcap_{k\in B}A_k^+\bigr)\cap\bigl(\bigcap_{k\notin B}(A_k^+)^{\mathrm{c}}\bigr)$ for each subset $B$ of such interior indices fixes the positive-side breakpoints, the number $U$ of pieces of positive width on the positive side, and the endpoints of every piece as fixed continuous vertex coordinates of the path. Performing the analogous finite intersection on the negative side fixes the number $L$ of negative-side pieces of positive width and their continuous endpoints.

Finally, on each cell of the combined finite Borel partition of $\mathscr{L}_{n,n^\prime}$, the numbers $L$ and $U$ are constant, and $\Psi(P_{\bm{m}})$ linearly interpolates the endpoints of the $j$th positive-width piece over $[(j-1)/U,j/U]$ on the positive side and over $[j/L,(j+1)/L]$ on the negative side. Recording the quantity endpoints and the one-sided price endpoints of every piece (retaining both price endpoints at a jump) yields a continuous finite-dimensional vector of endpoint coordinates on the cell. Because the map from this finite-dimensional vector of endpoint coordinates to the equivalence class of the corresponding piecewise-affine interpolant in $L^2([-1,1];\mathbb{R}^2)$ is linear and continuous, both $Z$ and $\bm{m}\mapsto P_{\bm{m}}(0)$ are continuous on every cell of the partition. Since the countable union of these finite partitions over $(n,n^\prime)\in\mathbb{N}^2$ forms a countable Borel partition of $\mathscr{L}$, Theorem~\ref{thm:measurable-partition} implies that $Z$ and $\bm{m}\mapsto P_{\bm{m}}(0)$ are Borel measurable on $\mathscr{L}$.

To prove the second claim, let $\bm{M}$ be a measurable random state in $\mathscr{L}$ with $\mathbb{E}[D(\bm{M})^2]<\infty$, and set $M_p=\max(500,\overline{p})$. Since every point of $\Psi(P_{\bm{M}})(u)$ lies on the completed graph of $P_{\bm{M}}$, we have $\lvert\psi_{P_{\bm{M}}}(u)\rvert\leq D(\bm{M})$ and $\lvert P_{\bm{M}}(\psi_{P_{\bm{M}}}(u))\rvert\leq M_p$ for all $u\in[-1,1]$. Integrating over $u\in[-1,1]$ yields
\begin{align*}
    \lVert Z(\bm{M})\rVert_{L^2([-1,1];\mathbb{R}^2)}^2
    =\int_{-1}^1\Bigl(\bigl\lvert\psi_{P_{\bm{M}}}(u)\bigr\rvert^2+\bigl\lvert P_{\bm{M}}\bigl(\psi_{P_{\bm{M}}}(u)\bigr)\bigr\rvert^2\Bigr)\,\mathrm{d}u
    \leq 2D(\bm{M})^2+2M_p^2.
\end{align*}
Taking expectations gives $\mathbb{E}[\lVert Z(\bm{M})\rVert_{L^2([-1,1];\mathbb{R}^2)}^2]\leq 2\mathbb{E}[D(\bm{M})^2]+2M_p^2<\infty$. Because $L^2([-1,1];\mathbb{R}^2)$ is separable, Borel measurability of $Z(\bm{M})$ implies strong measurability by Pettis's measurability theorem, so $Z(\bm{M})$ is a square-integrable Hilbert-valued random element, as desired.

