proof.rawEnvironment:
\begin{proof}
    We must show that the partial sums of the series $\sum_{n\in\mathbb{N}}\mathbb{E}[\theta_n\mid\mathcal{G}]e_n$ converge in $\mathcal{H}$ almost surely to $\mathbb{E}[X\mid\mathcal{G}]$. For almost every $\omega\in\Omega$, the orthonormal basis expansion gives
    \begin{align*}
        X(\omega) = \sum_{n \in \mathbb{N}} \theta_n(\omega) e_n,
    \end{align*}
    where $\theta_n(\omega)=\langle X(\omega),e_n\rangle$. Define the partial sums
    \begin{align*}
        S_N(\omega) = \sum_{n = 1}^N \theta_n(\omega) e_n,\qquad N\in\mathbb{N}.
    \end{align*}
    By the linearity of conditional expectation\index{conditional expectation} and the property that $\mathbb{E}[\theta_n e_n\mid\mathcal{G}]=\mathbb{E}[\theta_n\mid\mathcal{G}]e_n$ for a fixed deterministic basis vector $e_n$, it follows that
    \begin{align*}
        \mathbb{E}[S_N \mid \mathcal{G}] = \sum_{n=1}^N \mathbb{E}[\theta_n \mid \mathcal{G}] e_n\qquad\text{almost surely.}
    \end{align*}
    Moreover, because $S_N$ is the orthogonal projection of $X$ onto $\mathrm{span}\{e_1,\dots,e_N\}$, we have
    \begin{align*}
        S_N\to X,
        \qquad
        \lVert S_N\rVert\leq\lVert X\rVert,
        \quad\text{almost surely.}
    \end{align*}
    Since $\mathbb{E}[\lVert X\rVert]<\infty$ by the square-integrability hypothesis, the conditional dominated convergence theorem yields, almost surely,
    \begin{align*}
        \mathbb{E}[X \mid \mathcal{G}] = \lim_{N \to \infty} \mathbb{E}[S_N \mid \mathcal{G}] = \lim_{N \to \infty} \sum_{n = 1}^N \mathbb{E}[\theta_n \mid \mathcal{G}] e_n = \sum_{n=1}^\infty \mathbb{E}[\theta_n \mid \mathcal{G}] e_n,
    \end{align*}
    which establishes the coordinatewise representation of $\mathbb{E}[X\mid\mathcal{G}]$, as desired.
\end{proof}

