proof.rawEnvironment:
\begin{proof}
We establish the identities in \eqref{eq:compute-split-1} and \eqref{eq:compute-split-2}, rewrite them in terms of $\bm{G}$, and then bound the spectral-truncation error. For any non-empty $S\subseteq\{1,\dots,n\}$, expanding the squared norm in Definition~\ref{defn:cell-impurity-split} gives
\begin{align*}
    i(S) &= \frac{1}{|S|} \sum_{i \in S} \left\lVert y_i -\hat{y}_S\right\rVert_{\mathcal{H}}^2 \\
    &= \frac{1}{|S|} \sum_{i \in S}\lVert y_i\rVert_{\mathcal{H}}^2 - 2 \left\langle \frac{1}{|S|} \sum_{i \in S} y_i,\hat{y}_S \right\rangle_{\mathcal{H}} + \left\lVert\hat{y}_S\right\rVert_{\mathcal{H}}^2\\
    &=\frac{1}{|S|} \sum_{i\in S}\lVert y_i\rVert_{\mathcal{H}}^2 - \left\lVert\hat{y}_S\right\rVert_{\mathcal{H}}^2.
\end{align*}
Similarly, expanding $\frac{1}{2|S|^2}\sum_{i,j\in S}\lVert y_i-y_j\rVert_{\mathcal{H}}^2 = \frac{1}{2|S|^2}\sum_{i,j\in S}(\lVert y_i\rVert_{\mathcal{H}}^2 - 2\langle y_i,y_j\rangle_{\mathcal{H}} + \lVert y_j\rVert_{\mathcal{H}}^2)$ yields the second equality in \eqref{eq:compute-split-1}. Substituting the first equality of \eqref{eq:compute-split-1} into $\Delta i(s,S)$ and noting that $\sum_{i\in S}\lVert y_i\rVert_{\mathcal{H}}^2 = \sum_{i\in S_L}\lVert y_i\rVert_{\mathcal{H}}^2 + \sum_{i\in S_R}\lVert y_i\rVert_{\mathcal{H}}^2$ cancels the squared-norm sums, leaving
\begin{align*}
    \Delta i(s,S) = \frac{|S_L|}{|S|}\left\lVert\hat{y}_{S_L}\right\rVert_{\mathcal{H}}^2 + \frac{|S_R|}{|S|}\left\lVert\hat{y}_{S_R}\right\rVert_{\mathcal{H}}^2 - \left\lVert\hat{y}_S\right\rVert_{\mathcal{H}}^2.
\end{align*}
Since $\hat{y}_S = (|S_L|\hat{y}_{S_L} + |S_R|\hat{y}_{S_R})/|S|$ and $|S_L|+|S_R|=|S|$, expanding $\lVert\hat{y}_S\rVert_{\mathcal{H}}^2$ and collecting terms gives \eqref{eq:compute-split-2}. Because $\lVert y_i\rVert_{\mathcal{H}}^2=(\bm{G})_{i,i}$ and $\langle\sum_{i\in A}y_i,\sum_{j\in B}y_j\rangle_{\mathcal{H}}=\bm{1}_A^\top\bm{G}\bm{1}_B$ for all $A,B\subseteq\{1,\dots,n\}$, the Gram-matrix expressions for $i(S)$ and $\Delta i(s,S)$ follow immediately.

Finally, define the vector $\bm{w}\in\mathbb{R}^n$ by
\begin{align*}
    \bm{w} = \frac{\sqrt{|S_L||S_R|}}{|S|}\left(\frac{1}{|S_L|}\bm{1}_{S_L} - \frac{1}{|S_R|}\bm{1}_{S_R}\right).
\end{align*}
By construction, $\Delta i(s,S)=\bm{w}^\top\bm{G}\bm{w}$ and $\Delta\tilde{i}(s,S)=\bm{w}^\top\bm{G}_r\bm{w}$. Since $S_L$ and $S_R$ are disjoint, the Euclidean squared norm of $\bm{w}$ is
\begin{align*}
    \lVert\bm{w}\rVert_2^2 = \frac{|S_L||S_R|}{|S|^2}\left(\frac{|S_L|}{|S_L|^2} + \frac{|S_R|}{|S_R|^2}\right) = \frac{|S_L|+|S_R|}{|S|^2} = \frac{1}{|S|}.
\end{align*}
The residual matrix $\bm{G}-\bm{G}_r$ is symmetric positive semidefinite with largest eigenvalue $\lambda_{r+1}$, so the Rayleigh quotient bound gives $0 \leq \bm{w}^\top(\bm{G}-\bm{G}_r)\bm{w} \leq \lambda_{r+1}\lVert\bm{w}\rVert_2^2 = \lambda_{r+1}/|S|$, completing the proof.
\end{proof}

