\begin{proof}
We must show that $\mathcal{C}_q$ and $\mathcal{C}_p$ are non-empty, closed, and convex subsets, that $\mathbb{E}[W\mid\mathcal{F}_d]\in\mathcal{C}_q\times\mathcal{C}_p$ almost surely whenever $W$ is Bochner integrable and takes values in $\mathcal{C}_q\times\mathcal{C}_p$ almost surely, and that componentwise metric projection does not increase the distance to any $Y\in\mathcal{C}_q\times\mathcal{C}_p$. Because the monotonicity constraints $z_{i}\leq z_{i+1}$ and the box constraints $\underline{p}\leq p_j\leq\overline{p}$ are finite systems of closed linear inequalities, both $\mathcal{C}_q$ and $\mathcal{C}_p$ are non-empty, closed, and convex polyhedra. Next, Bochner integrability of $W$ implies the integrability of every scalar coordinate. Because coordinate projections commute with Bochner conditional expectation and scalar conditional expectation preserves each adjacent-coordinate inequality and each price bound almost surely, intersecting the finitely many corresponding full-measure events shows that $\mathbb{E}[W\mid\mathcal{F}_d]$ takes values in $\mathcal{C}_q\times\mathcal{C}_p$ almost surely. Finally, because the metric projection onto a non-empty, closed, and convex subset of a Hilbert space is non-expansive, the distance to every point $Y\in\mathcal{C}_q\times\mathcal{C}_p$ cannot increase under componentwise projection, as desired.
\end{proof}

