\label{prop:battery-sublinear-scaling}
Let $\mathcal{G}$, $\mathcal{R}$, and $\tau$ be as in Definition~\ref{defn:battery-schedule-set}, let $\lambda\geq 0$, and let $\widehat{\bm{P}}_d = (\widehat{P}_{d,h})_{h=1}^{24}$ be a $24$-tuple of non-decreasing functions on intervals $\operatorname{domain}(\widehat{P}_{d,h})\subseteq\mathbb{R}$ such that $0\in\operatorname{domain}(\widehat{P}_{d,h})$ for each $h\in\{1,\dots,24\}$. For any scaling factor $\alpha > 1$ and $q_{\mathrm{start}},q_{\mathrm{stop}}\in\mathcal{G}$, let $\mathcal{A}_{\alpha\mathcal{G}}(\alpha q_{\mathrm{start}}, \alpha q_{\mathrm{stop}}; \widehat{\bm{P}}_d)$ denote the feasible schedule set obtained by replacing $\mathcal{G}$ and $\mathcal{R}$ with $\alpha\mathcal{G}$ and $\alpha\mathcal{R}$, and let $\widehat{V}_{d,\alpha}(\alpha q_{\mathrm{start}}, \alpha q_{\mathrm{stop}})$ denote the corresponding optimal value of \eqref{eq:approximate-battery-problem}. If $\mathcal{A}_{\alpha\mathcal{G}}(\alpha q_{\mathrm{start}}, \alpha q_{\mathrm{stop}}; \widehat{\bm{P}}_d)$ is non-empty, then $\mathcal{A}_{\mathcal{G}}(q_{\mathrm{start}}, q_{\mathrm{stop}}; \widehat{\bm{P}}_d)$ is also non-empty and
\begin{align*}
    \widehat{V}_{d,\alpha}(\alpha q_{\mathrm{start}}, \alpha q_{\mathrm{stop}}) \leq \alpha\,\widehat{V}_d(q_{\mathrm{start}}, q_{\mathrm{stop}}).
\end{align*}

