proof.rawEnvironment:
\begin{proof}
    Let $\bm{m} \in \mathscr{S}(O, \preceq)$ be given, and for each $\kappa\in\{\mathrm{buy},\mathrm{sell}\}$, define the side index set
    \begin{align*}
        I_\kappa(\bm{m})=\{i\in\operatorname{domain}(\bm{m}) \mid \bm{m}(i) \in \left([-9999,9999]\times (0,\infty) \times [0,1]\right) \times \{\kappa\}\},
    \end{align*}
    and let $n=\ell(\bm{\Pi}_{\mathrm{buy}}\bm{m})$. If a sell order occurred at an index $i$ before a buy order at an index $j$, then $i<j$ and $\bm{m}(j)\prec\bm{m}(i)$ by \eqref{eq:price-time-priority}, contradicting Definition~\ref{defn:sorted-tuples}. Hence,
    \begin{align*}
        I_{\mathrm{buy}}(\bm{m})=\{1,\ldots,n\}
        \qquad\text{and}\qquad
        I_{\mathrm{sell}}(\bm{m})=\{n+1,\ldots,\ell(\bm{m})\}.
    \end{align*}
    Fix $\kappa\in\{\mathrm{buy},\mathrm{sell}\}$, and let $\prec^\kappa$ denote the strict part of $\preceq^\kappa$. Let $r,r^\prime\in\operatorname{domain}(\bm{\Pi}_\kappa\bm{m})$ satisfy $r<r^\prime$. Since $\bm{\Pi}_\kappa\bm{m}=\bm{P}_{\mathrm{element}}(\bm{m}[I_\kappa(\bm{m})])$ by Definition~\ref{defn:separation-operators}, reindexing gives indices $i<j$ in $I_\kappa(\bm{m})$ such that
    \begin{align*}
        (\bm{\Pi}_\kappa\bm{m})(r)=o_i,\qquad
        (\bm{\Pi}_\kappa\bm{m})(r^\prime)=o_j,
        \qquad
        \bm{m}(i)=(o_i,\kappa),\qquad
        \bm{m}(j)=(o_j,\kappa).
    \end{align*}
    Since $\bm{m}$ is sorted, $\neg(\bm{m}(j)\prec\bm{m}(i))$. By \eqref{eq:price-time-priority}, this gives $\neg(o_j\prec^\kappa o_i)$. Consequently, no later entry of $\bm{\Pi}_\kappa\bm{m}$ strictly precedes an earlier entry, which establishes that $\bm{\Pi}_\kappa\bm{m} \in \mathscr{S}([-9999,9999]\times (0,\infty) \times [0,1], \preceq^\kappa)$ by Definition~\ref{defn:sorted-tuples}, as claimed.
\end{proof}

