        \Phi^{(i)} : \mathscr{S}(U,\preceq^{\mathrm{buy}})\times\mathscr{S}(U,\preceq^{\mathrm{sell}}) \to \mathscr{S}(U,\preceq^{\mathrm{buy}})\times\mathscr{S}(U,\preceq^{\mathrm{sell}})
    \end{align*}
    is defined recursively by
    \begin{align*}
        \Phi^{(0)} &= \mathrm{Id},\\
        \Phi^{(1)} &= \Phi, \\
        \Phi^{(i)} &= \Phi^{(i-1)} \circ \Phi, \qquad i > 1.
    \end{align*}

proof.rawEnvironment:
\begin{proof}
    We proceed by induction on $i \in \mathbb{N}_0$. For $i = 0$, $\Phi^{(0)} = \mathrm{Id}$ is the identity map on $\mathscr{S}(U,\preceq^{\mathrm{buy}})\times\mathscr{S}(U,\preceq^{\mathrm{sell}})$, which is continuous and hence Borel measurable. For $i = 1$, $\Phi^{(1)} = \Phi$ is Borel measurable by Proposition~\ref{prop:single-stage-execution}. Suppose now that $\Phi^{(i-1)}$ is Borel measurable for some $i > 1$. Since $\Phi^{(i)} = \Phi^{(i-1)} \circ \Phi$ and the composition of Borel measurable maps is Borel measurable, it follows that $\Phi^{(i)}$ is Borel measurable. By induction, $\Phi^{(i)}$ is Borel measurable for all $i \in \mathbb{N}_0$.
\end{proof}

