    Let $(\Omega,\mathcal{F},\mathbb{P})$ be a probability space, let $\mathscr{C}(O)$ be the composite-action space over a complete and separable metric space $(O,d_O)$, and let $(\xi_k)_{k=1}^K$ be independent and identically distributed point processes in $\mathcal{N}([0,1]\times\mathscr{C}(O))$ such that, for each $k\in\{1,\dots,K\}$, $\xi_k(\{0\}\times\mathscr{C}(O))=0$ almost surely and the block restrictions $\xi_k^1,\dots,\xi_k^L$ of Definition~\ref{defn:block-bootstrap} are mutually independent:
    \begin{align*}
        \mathbb{P}\left(\xi_k^1 \in A_1, \dots, \xi_k^L \in A_L\right) = \prod_{j = 1}^L \mathbb{P}\left(\xi_k^j \in A_j\right)
    \end{align*}
    for all $A_1, \dots, A_L \in \mathcal{B}(\mathcal{N}([0,1]\times \mathscr{C}(O)))$. Then, for any deterministic sequence $(k_j)_{j=1}^L\in\{1,\dots,K\}^L$, or any random sequence $(k_j)_{j=1}^L$ taking values in $\{1,\dots,K\}^L$ independently of $(\xi_k)_{k=1}^K$, the block-bootstrapped point process $\xi^\ast = \sum_{j=1}^L \xi_{k_j}^j$ satisfies $\xi^\ast \stackrel{d}{=} \xi_1$.

