\begin{proof}
    We must show that the map $\Phi\colon X_1\times X_2\to\prod_{i=1}^2 X_i$ given by $\Phi(x_1,x_2)=\{(1,x_1),(2,x_2)\}$ is well-defined and admits a two-sided inverse. For any $(x_1,x_2)\in X_1\times X_2$, the set $\{(1,x_1),(2,x_2)\}$ is a function on $\{1,2\}$ taking values $x_1\in X_1$ at $1$ and $x_2\in X_2$ at $2$, so $\Phi(x_1,x_2)\in\prod_{i=1}^2X_i$. Define $\Psi\colon\prod_{i=1}^2X_i\to X_1\times X_2$ by $\Psi(z)=(z(1),z(2))$. Then $\Psi(\Phi(x_1,x_2))=(x_1,x_2)$ for every $(x_1,x_2)\in X_1\times X_2$, and for every $z\in\prod_{i=1}^2X_i$, Proposition~\ref{prop:graph-defines} gives $\Phi(\Psi(z))=\{(1,z(1)),(2,z(2))\}=\Gamma(z)=z$. Thus $\Phi$ is a bijection, as desired.
\end{proof}

