\begin{proof}
    We must show that $\sim$ is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive on $X\times X$, and that it is compatible with componentwise addition. Reflexivity ($x+y=x+y$) and symmetry ($x+y^\prime=x^\prime+y\iff x^\prime+y=x+y^\prime$) are immediate from commutativity. For transitivity, suppose that $(x,y)\sim(x^\prime,y^\prime)$ and $(x^\prime,y^\prime)\sim(x^\ast,y^\ast)$. Associativity, commutativity, and the two defining equalities give
    \begin{align*}
        (x+y^\ast)+y^\prime&=(x+y^\prime)+y^\ast=(x^\prime+y)+y^\ast\\
        &=(x^\prime+y^\ast)+y=(x^\ast+y^\prime)+y=(x^\ast+y)+y^\prime,
    \end{align*}
    and cancelling $y^\prime$ yields $x+y^\ast=x^\ast+y$, so $(x,y)\sim(x^\ast,y^\ast)$. For the second claim, suppose that $(x,y)\sim(x^\prime,y^\prime)$ and $(u,v)\sim(u^\prime,v^\prime)$; then
    \begin{align*}
        (x+u)+(y^\prime+v^\prime)=(x+y^\prime)+(u+v^\prime)=(x^\prime+y)+(u^\prime+v)=(x^\prime+u^\prime)+(y+v),
    \end{align*}
    which proves that $(x+u,y+v)\sim(x^\prime+u^\prime,y^\prime+v^\prime)$, as desired.
\end{proof}

