\begin{rem}[When group completion applies]\label{rem:group-completion-scope}
    In Chapter~\ref{chap:hilbert-market-representations}, we keep several constructions distinct. The kernel quotient in Definition~\ref{defn:function-kernel-quotient-space} handles non-injectivity by identifying objects with the same encoded value. Before applying the construction of Definition~\ref{defn:grothendieck-group}, we verify that the relevant transported object is closed under its proposed addition and is a cancellative abelian monoid. Failure of closure under that addition is not repaired by quotienting. If cancellation fails, the relation of Proposition~\ref{prop:grothendieck-relation} must be replaced by the general Grothendieck congruence, $(x,y)\approx(x^\prime,y^\prime)$ if and only if $x+y^\prime+k=x^\prime+y+k$ for some $k\in X$, or by an explicitly defined quotient that enforces cancellation, and the resulting canonical map need not be injective. Theorem~\ref{thm:grothendieck-vector-space-from-convex-cone} introduces formal differences, and Corollary~\ref{thm:grothendieck-vectorization-is-span} identifies the resulting vector space with $\mathrm{span}~\varphi(X)$ rather than with the whole ambient space. Definition~\ref{defn:completed-kernel-space} and Corollary~\ref{cor:grothendieck-vectorization-inner-product-space} use metric completion to add limits of Cauchy sequences; this is distinct from group completion. None of these constructions makes a formal inverse a feasible market cancellation, trade, execution, or state transition.
\end{rem}

\begin{defn}[{Field \citep[Chapter~II]{lang2012algebra}}]\label{defn:field}
    Let $\mathbb{K}$ be a set with two binary operations $+:\mathbb{K}\times\mathbb{K}\to\mathbb{K}$ and $\cdot:\mathbb{K}\times\mathbb{K}\to\mathbb{K}$ such that
    \begin{enumerate}[(i)]
        \item $(\mathbb{K},+)$ is an abelian group, whose identity element we denote by $0$;
        \item $\cdot$ maps $(\mathbb{K}\setminus\{0\})\times(\mathbb{K}\setminus\{0\})$ into $\mathbb{K}\setminus\{0\}$, and $(\mathbb{K}\setminus\{0\},\cdot)$ is an abelian group, whose identity element we denote by $1$ and in which we write $a^{-1}$ for the inverse of $a$;
        \item $a\cdot(b+c)=a\cdot b+a\cdot c$ and $(a+b)\cdot c=a\cdot c+b\cdot c$ for all $a,b,c\in\mathbb{K}$.
    \end{enumerate}
    Then $(\mathbb{K},+,\cdot)$ is called a field.
\end{defn}

