\begin{defn}[{Vector Space \citep[Chapter~III]{lang2012algebra}}] \label{defn:real-vector-space}
    Let $(V, +)$ be an abelian group and let $\mathbb{K}$ be a field. Let $\cdot: \mathbb{K} \times V \to V$ be a function that satisfies
    \begin{enumerate}[(i)]
        \item $1\cdot v = v$ for all $v \in V$.
        \item $a\cdot(u + v) = a\cdot u + a\cdot v$ for all $u,v\in V$ and $a \in \mathbb{K}$.
        \item $(a + b)\cdot v = a\cdot v + b \cdot v$ for all $v\in V$ and $a,b\in \mathbb{K}$.
        \item $a\cdot(b\cdot v) = (a\cdot b)\cdot v$ for all $v \in V$ and $a,b\in \mathbb{K}$.
    \end{enumerate}
    Then $(V, +, \cdot)$ is called a vector space over $\mathbb{K}$. A vector space over $\mathbb{R}$ is called a real vector space.
\end{defn}

proof.rawEnvironment:
\begin{proof}
    We must show that $(F(X;V),+,\cdot)$ is an abelian group under pointwise addition and satisfies the four vector-space axioms (i)--(iv) of Definition~\ref{defn:real-vector-space}. All axioms are inherited pointwise from $V$. Specifically, pointwise addition is associative and commutative because addition in $V$ is associative and commutative, the zero function $0(x)=0_V$ is the additive identity, the pointwise inverse is given by $(-f)(x)=-f(x)$, and the scalar-multiplication axioms (i)--(iv) follow by evaluating the corresponding identities in $V$ at each $x \in X$.
    In the degenerate case where $X=\emptyset$, Proposition~\ref{prop:empty-domain-function} gives the unique empty-domain function, so $F(X;V)=\{\emptyset\}$ and the pointwise operations make it the zero vector space, as desired.
\end{proof}

