proof.rawEnvironment:
\begin{proof}
    We verify the two defining properties. Let $(x,y) \in X \times Y$. Since $\mathcal{B}_X$ and $\mathcal{B}_Y$ are bases, there exist $B_X \in \mathcal{B}_X$ and $B_Y \in \mathcal{B}_Y$ such that $x \in B_X$ and $y \in B_Y$. Hence $(x,y) \in B_X \times B_Y \in \mathcal{B}_X \otimes \mathcal{B}_Y$.

    Now let $B = B_X \times B_Y$ and $B^\prime = B_X^\prime \times B_Y^\prime$ be elements of $\mathcal{B}_X \otimes \mathcal{B}_Y$, and suppose $(x,y) \in B \cap B^\prime$. Then $x \in B_X \cap B_X^\prime$ and $y \in B_Y \cap B_Y^\prime$. By the base property, there exist $B_X^\ast \in \mathcal{B}_X$ and $B_Y^\ast \in \mathcal{B}_Y$ such that
    \begin{align*}
        x \in B_X^\ast \subseteq B_X \cap B_X^\prime
        \qquad \text{and} \qquad
        y \in B_Y^\ast \subseteq B_Y \cap B_Y^\prime.
    \end{align*}
    Therefore
    \begin{align*}
        (x,y) \in B_X^\ast \times B_Y^\ast \subseteq (B_X \times B_Y) \cap (B_X^\prime \times B_Y^\prime) = B \cap B^\prime,
    \end{align*}
    and $B_X^\ast \times B_Y^\ast \in \mathcal{B}_X \otimes \mathcal{B}_Y$.
\end{proof}

