\begin{proof}
    We must show that $\sim$ is an equivalence relation, that $d_\sim$ is independent of the chosen representatives, and that $(X_\sim,d_\sim)$ satisfies the metric axioms. First, reflexivity and symmetry of $\sim$ follow immediately from the pseudometric axioms~(\ref{eq:pseudometric-1}) and~(\ref{eq:pseudometric-2}), while $x\sim y$ and $y\sim z$ imply $0\leq d(x,z)\leq d(x,y)+d(y,z)=0$, which proves transitivity. Next, if $x\sim y$ and $x^\ast\sim y^\ast$, then the triangle inequality gives
    \begin{align*}
        d(x,x^\ast)\leq d(x,y)+d(y,y^\ast)+d(y^\ast,x^\ast)=d(y,y^\ast),
    \end{align*}
    and exchanging the two representative pairs gives the reverse inequality $d(y,y^\ast)\leq d(x,x^\ast)$. Hence $d(x,x^\ast)=d(y,y^\ast)$, so $d_\sim$ is well-defined. Finally, symmetry and the triangle inequality descend directly from $d$, and $d_\sim([x],[x^\ast])=0$ implies $x\sim x^\ast$ and hence $[x]=[x^\ast]$ by definition of the quotient, as desired.
\end{proof}

