        d(x,y) < \varepsilon.
    \end{align*}

environments.rawEnvironment:
\begin{defn}[Polish space]
    Let $(X,\mathcal{T})$ be a topological space. Then $(X,\mathcal{T})$ is called Polish if there exists a metric on $X$ that generates $\mathcal{T}$ and for which the resulting metric space is complete and separable.
\end{defn}

\begin{defn}[$G_\delta$ set]\label{defn:g-delta}\index{G-delta set@$G_\delta$ set}
    Let $(X,\mathcal{T})$ be a topological space. A subset $A \subseteq X$ is called a $G_\delta$ set if there exist open sets $U_1, U_2, \dots \in \mathcal{T}$ such that $A = \bigcap_{n\in\mathbb{N}} U_n$.
\end{defn}

\begin{thm}[{Alexandrov; \citealp[Theorem~3.11]{kechris1995classical}}]\label{thm:alexandrov}\index{Alexandrov's theorem}
    Let $(X,\mathcal{T})$ be a Polish space and let $Y \subseteq X$. Then $Y$, endowed with the subspace topology, is Polish if and only if $Y$ is a $G_\delta$ set in $X$.
\end{thm}

\begin{defn}[Standard Borel space]\label{defn:standard-borel-space}\index{standard Borel space}
    A measurable space $(X,\Sigma)$ is called standard Borel if there exists a Polish topology $\mathcal{T}$ on $X$ such that $\Sigma=\mathcal{B}(X,\mathcal{T})$, the Borel $\sigma$-algebra generated by $\mathcal{T}$ (Appendix~\ref{app:measure-theory}; not to be confused with a topological base $\mathcal{B}$).
\end{defn}

\begin{thm}[{\citealp[Corollary~13.4]{kechris1995classical}}]\label{thm:borel-subset-standard-borel}
    Let $(X,\Sigma)$ be a standard Borel space and let $Y \in \Sigma$. Then $Y$, endowed with the trace $\sigma$-algebra $\{A \cap Y \mid A \in \Sigma\}$, is a standard Borel space.
\end{thm}

proof.rawEnvironment:
\begin{proof}
    We must show that the maximum map defines a pseudometric (and a metric when each $d_i$ is a metric) that induces the product topology and inherits separability and completeness from the factors. The maximum vanishes on the diagonal and is symmetric because every $d_i$ is, and the triangle inequality holds in each coordinate and therefore for the maximum; if every $d_i$ is a metric, vanishing of the maximum implies equality in every coordinate. Next, the ball of radius $\varepsilon$ around $\bm{x}$ is $\prod_{i=1}^n B^{d_i}_\varepsilon(\bm{x}(i))$, which is open in the product topology. Conversely, if $\bm{x}\in\prod_{i=1}^n O_i$ with each $O_i$ open in $X_i$, choose $\varepsilon_i>0$ with $B^{d_i}_{\varepsilon_i}(\bm{x}(i))\subseteq O_i$; the ball of radius $\min_i\varepsilon_i$ around $\bm{x}$ then lies in $\prod_{i=1}^n O_i$. By the centred-ball characterisation following Proposition~\ref{prop:topology-extended-pseudometric}, the two topologies coincide.

    Finally, if $D_i\subseteq X_i$ is countable and dense for each $i$, then $\prod_{i=1}^n D_i$ is countable and dense, since for $\bm{x}$ and $\varepsilon>0$ we may choose $y_i\in D_i$ with $d_i(\bm{x}(i),y_i)<\varepsilon$ for every $i$. If $(\bm{x}_m)_{m\in\mathbb{N}}$ is Cauchy, then so is each coordinate sequence. If every factor is complete, their limits $x_i\in X_i$ form $\bm{x}=(x_1,\dots,x_n)$, and $\bm{x}_m\to\bm{x}$ in every coordinate and hence in the maximum metric, as desired.
\end{proof}

environments.rawEnvironment:
\begin{prop}[Finite metric products]\label{prop:finite-metric-products}
    Let $n\in\mathbb{N}$ and let $(X_1,d_1),\dots,(X_n,d_n)$ be pseudometric spaces. Then
    \begin{align*}
        (\bm{x},\bm{x}^\prime)\mapsto\max_{i\in\{1,\dots,n\}}d_i(\bm{x}(i),\bm{x}^\prime(i)),\qquad \bm{x},\bm{x}^\prime\in\prod_{i=1}^n X_i,
    \end{align*}
    is a pseudometric on $\prod_{i=1}^n X_i$, called the maximum metric\index{product metric}, and it is a metric if every $d_i$ is. If every $d_i$ is a metric, then the maximum metric induces the product topology of the topologies induced by $d_1,\dots,d_n$, and the product is separable if every factor is separable and complete if every factor is complete.
\end{prop}

