proof.rawEnvironment:
\begin{proof}
    We must show that $\Gamma(f)$ is compact, closed, and bounded in $([a,b]\times X,\rho)$. Define $\Phi:[a,b] \to [a,b] \times X$ by $\Phi(t) = (t,f(t))$. Continuity of $f$ implies that $\Phi$ is continuous under $\rho$. The interval $[a,b]$ is compact, so its continuous image $\Phi([a,b]) = \Gamma(f)$ is compact. Every metric space in the sense of Remark~\ref{rem:extended-metric-convention} is Hausdorff, so the compact set $\Gamma(f)$ is closed. The finite-distance components of $\rho$ (Remark~\ref{rem:extended-metric-convention}) are both open and closed, and $\Gamma(f)$ is connected as the continuous image of the connected interval $[a,b]$; hence it lies in one such component. On that component, $\rho$ is an ordinary metric, so compactness implies that $\Gamma(f)$ is bounded, as desired.
\end{proof}

environments.rawEnvironment:
\begin{prop}[Graph of a continuous function is compact]
    Let $a,b \in \mathbb{R}$ with $a < b$, let $(X,d)$ be a metric space, and let $f \in C([a,b];X)$. Then the graph
    \begin{align*}
        \Gamma(f) = \{(t,f(t)) \mid t \in [a,b]\}
    \end{align*}
    is compact in $[a,b] \times X$ under the metric
    \begin{align*}
        \rho((s,x),(t,y)) = |s-t| + d(x,y).
    \end{align*}
    In particular, $\Gamma(f)$ is closed and bounded.
\end{prop}

