proof.rawEnvironment:
\begin{proof}
We must show that for every $\hat{y}\in\mathcal{H}$, the linear functional $A\colon\mathcal{H}\to\mathbb{R}$ defined by $Ah=2\langle\hat{y}-y,h\rangle$ belongs to $\mathcal{H}^\ast$ and satisfies Definition~\ref{defn:frechet-derivative}. For any $\hat{y},h\in\mathcal{H}$, expanding the squared norm yields
\begin{align*}
    \mathfrak{f}(\hat{y} + h) - \mathfrak{f}(\hat{y}) = \lVert y - \hat{y} - h\rVert^2 - \lVert y - \hat{y}\rVert^2 = 2\langle \hat{y} - y,h\rangle + \lVert h\rVert^2.
\end{align*}
The map $Ah=2\langle \hat{y} - y, h\rangle$ is linear in $h$ and bounded by the Cauchy--Schwarz inequality ($\lvert Ah\rvert\leq 2\lVert\hat{y}-y\rVert\,\lVert h\rVert$), and for $h\neq 0_{\mathcal{H}}$,
\begin{align*}
    \frac{\lvert\mathfrak{f}(\hat{y}+h) - \mathfrak{f}(\hat{y}) - Ah\rvert}{\lVert h\rVert} = \lVert h\rVert \to 0 \quad\text{as } \lVert h\rVert\to 0.
\end{align*}
Consequently, $D\mathfrak{f}(\hat{y})=A$, whose Riesz representer is $\nabla\mathfrak{f}(\hat{y})=2(\hat{y}-y)$, as desired.
\end{proof}

