\begin{proof}
    We must show that for any $f$ in $C([a,b];V)$, $D^+([a,b];V)$, or $D^-([a,b];V)$, the function $f$ is Borel measurable and satisfies $\int_a^b \lVert f(t)\rVert^2\,\mathrm{d}\lambda(t)<\infty$. By Lemma~\ref{lem:regular-paths-bounded-borel}, every continuous, c\`adl\`ag, or c\`agl\`ad Banach-valued function on the compact interval $[a,b]$ is Borel measurable and bounded. Since $\lambda([a,b])=b-a<\infty$, the bounded function $t\mapsto \lVert f(t)\rVert^2$ is Lebesgue integrable on $[a,b]$, and hence $[f]\in L^2([a,b],\lambda;V)$.
\end{proof}

